1
00:00:01,001 --> 00:00:04,213
[grand orchestral fanfare playing]

2
00:00:31,240 --> 00:00:33,242
- [birds chirping]
- [insects trilling]

3
00:00:57,308 --> 00:00:58,434
[bird squawks]

4
00:00:59,226 --> 00:01:01,937
[monkeys chattering]

5
00:01:02,021 --> 00:01:05,232
[low, deep rumbling]

6
00:01:06,567 --> 00:01:08,360
- [monkey squawks]
- [leaves rustling]

7
00:01:08,444 --> 00:01:11,405
[rumbling grows louder]

8
00:01:13,157 --> 00:01:14,158
[monkey screeches]

9
00:01:39,475 --> 00:01:41,435
[scientist 1] Let me run
the new sequencing.

10
00:01:41,519 --> 00:01:43,187
I'll need a few minutes.

11
00:01:43,270 --> 00:01:44,647
[radio static crackles]

12
00:01:44,730 --> 00:01:46,732
How many times
are we going to keep doing this?

13
00:01:46,816 --> 00:01:49,693
[scientist 2] We keep running it
until the hybrid holds.

14
00:01:49,777 --> 00:01:51,779
[announcer 1 on PA]
<i>We are on security level five.</i>

15
00:01:51,862 --> 00:01:54,240
[scientist 1] The deregs took.
No sign of rejection.

16
00:01:55,407 --> 00:01:57,493
There's still mutations in genome seven.

17
00:01:57,576 --> 00:01:58,702
Thank you.

18
00:01:58,786 --> 00:02:00,788
[announcer 1 on PA]
<i>All genetic waste must be taken</i>

19
00:02:00,871 --> 00:02:02,998
<i>to the cremation chamber by 5:00 p. m.</i>

20
00:02:04,250 --> 00:02:08,879
[announcer 2 on PA] <i>Tank 12 is offline</i>
<i>until genetic modification stabilizes.</i>

21
00:02:14,677 --> 00:02:16,470
All right. Let's get this over with.

22
00:02:16,971 --> 00:02:18,055
Here we go.

23
00:02:20,391 --> 00:02:23,143
Oh. Yeah, sorry. Hold on.

24
00:02:24,937 --> 00:02:27,022
Okay. Yeah.

25
00:02:27,106 --> 00:02:30,651
[keypad beeping]

26
00:02:30,734 --> 00:02:33,279
Three, two, one.

27
00:02:38,367 --> 00:02:40,619
[scientist 3] Step up here.
Waiting for authorization.

28
00:02:40,703 --> 00:02:41,871
[scientist 4 sighs]

29
00:02:42,580 --> 00:02:46,208
I didn't see the labs, but I assume
we upped the soporifics?

30
00:02:46,292 --> 00:02:47,293
[door buzzes]

31
00:02:47,376 --> 00:02:50,671
Yeah. I'm gonna have to go as high
as.5 millimeters per 100 pounds.

32
00:02:50,754 --> 00:02:52,548
[scientist 3] Okay, guys, you're clear.

33
00:02:54,091 --> 00:02:56,051
Well, we don't wanna kill the thing.

34
00:02:57,052 --> 00:02:58,220
Some days I do.

35
00:02:59,555 --> 00:03:01,390
[sighs]

36
00:03:01,473 --> 00:03:02,808
Oh, God.

37
00:03:02,892 --> 00:03:06,979
[announcer 2 on PA] <i>Tank 12 is offline</i>
<i>until genetic modification stabilizes.</i>

38
00:03:07,062 --> 00:03:08,856
[air whooshing]

39
00:03:10,190 --> 00:03:11,442
[crackling]

40
00:03:11,525 --> 00:03:13,277
[powers down]

41
00:03:15,696 --> 00:03:17,907
[computer alarm beeping quietly]

42
00:03:24,622 --> 00:03:25,831
[alarm stops]

43
00:03:26,665 --> 00:03:27,666
[mechanical clanking]

44
00:03:27,750 --> 00:03:29,793
[automated voice]
<i>Alert. Containment failure.</i>

45
00:03:29,877 --> 00:03:31,962
- Hey, what's going on?
<i>- Alert. Containment failure.</i>

46
00:03:32,046 --> 00:03:33,172
[low growling]

47
00:03:34,006 --> 00:03:35,716
<i>Alert. Containment failure.</i>

48
00:03:35,799 --> 00:03:38,552
[deep guttural clicking]

49
00:03:38,636 --> 00:03:39,970
Oh, God. It's loose!

50
00:03:40,054 --> 00:03:42,097
- [alarm blaring]
- [urgent chattering]

51
00:03:43,557 --> 00:03:46,018
[scientist 5] Run!
Containment is locking down!

52
00:03:46,101 --> 00:03:48,687
- Go, go!
- The D-Rex broke free!

53
00:03:48,771 --> 00:03:49,772
Clear the building!

54
00:03:50,314 --> 00:03:52,441
[scientist 6] Seal the doors!
Seal the doors!

55
00:03:52,524 --> 00:03:53,567
- [screams]
- Let's go.

56
00:03:55,027 --> 00:03:56,111
We gotta get out of here.

57
00:03:56,195 --> 00:03:58,364
Come on. Go, go, go!

58
00:03:58,447 --> 00:04:00,616
- DeSantos! The door!
- Oh, God. Oh, God.

59
00:04:00,699 --> 00:04:02,743
Oh, no! No, no, no!

60
00:04:02,826 --> 00:04:04,536
- Come on! No.
- No, no, no! No!

61
00:04:04,620 --> 00:04:06,389
- Okay, it just... it won't work...
- It's okay.

62
00:04:06,413 --> 00:04:07,831
- Come on. Come on!
- Okay.

63
00:04:07,915 --> 00:04:10,042
Three, two, one.

64
00:04:11,168 --> 00:04:12,252
[breath trembles]

65
00:04:12,336 --> 00:04:14,213
Come on! Three, two...

66
00:04:14,296 --> 00:04:16,340
- Come on!
- [gasps softly]

67
00:04:18,342 --> 00:04:20,803
[deep growling]

68
00:04:21,679 --> 00:04:24,306
Come on! Open the door! Please!

69
00:04:24,390 --> 00:04:26,475
[heavy footstep booms]

70
00:04:26,558 --> 00:04:27,768
[growling]

71
00:04:27,851 --> 00:04:29,061
Please.

72
00:04:29,144 --> 00:04:30,938
[whispering] Okay, just turn the key.

73
00:04:31,021 --> 00:04:33,649
It's fine. It's okay. Just turn the key.

74
00:04:33,732 --> 00:04:36,735
- Just turn... Just please open the door.
- I'm sorry.

75
00:04:36,819 --> 00:04:40,948
[crying] Please open the door for me.

76
00:04:41,031 --> 00:04:42,116
- [screams]
- No!

77
00:04:43,909 --> 00:04:46,704
- [whimpering]
- [roaring]

78
00:04:47,413 --> 00:04:49,123
[crying] Oh, God! No.

79
00:04:49,206 --> 00:04:51,333
[pained screaming]

80
00:04:51,417 --> 00:04:53,502
[roaring]

81
00:04:53,585 --> 00:04:56,839
[crying] Oh, no! I'm sorry!

82
00:04:56,922 --> 00:04:59,800
[screaming] Oh, no!

83
00:04:59,883 --> 00:05:02,302
[dramatic music playing]

84
00:05:55,272 --> 00:05:57,066
[horns honking]

85
00:05:58,067 --> 00:05:59,485
[driver 1] Come on, what is this?

86
00:05:59,568 --> 00:06:02,338
[driver 2] These things were supposed
to be completely cleared two years ago.

87
00:06:02,362 --> 00:06:04,507
[officer] Yeah, well, I'm not happy
about it either, ma'am.

88
00:06:04,531 --> 00:06:06,051
They're trying to get him up right now.

89
00:06:06,075 --> 00:06:08,428
I don't know what you want me to tell you.
This is where we're at.

90
00:06:08,452 --> 00:06:10,680
[driver 3] What does the mayor do?
Sit on his ass all day?

91
00:06:10,704 --> 00:06:12,724
[officer] It weighs 40 tons, buddy.
Do you want to move it?

92
00:06:12,748 --> 00:06:15,834
[on radio] ♪ <i>Twelve o'clock radio ♪</i>

93
00:06:15,918 --> 00:06:17,461
[radio host]..<i>. out of Manhattan.</i>

94
00:06:17,544 --> 00:06:20,839
<i>The cause of the jam
is none other than Bronto Billy,</i>

95
00:06:20,923 --> 00:06:24,593
<i>thought to be the last surviving sauropod
in North America.</i>

96
00:06:24,676 --> 00:06:26,220
<i>- Back to you, Bob.
- Thanks, Hailey.</i>

97
00:06:26,303 --> 00:06:30,182
<i>So, yeah, the aging Brachiosaur,
known to be in poor health,</i>

98
00:06:30,265 --> 00:06:32,351
<i>broke out of
the Cobble Hill Park enclosure</i>

99
00:06:32,434 --> 00:06:34,704
<i>where it had been living
while receiving medical attention.</i>

100
00:06:34,728 --> 00:06:37,439
- [dinosaur bellowing]
- [horns honking]

101
00:06:41,193 --> 00:06:42,986
[chattering]

102
00:06:43,070 --> 00:06:44,613
[worker] Gonna need a bigger crane!

103
00:06:48,659 --> 00:06:49,868
Oh, for God's sake.

104
00:06:50,577 --> 00:06:53,122
Rest in peace already,
but get the hell out of the way.

105
00:06:53,622 --> 00:06:54,623
Damn, that's cold.

106
00:06:54,706 --> 00:06:55,874
Geez!

107
00:06:55,958 --> 00:06:58,544
I mean, even for New York,
it's pretty cold.

108
00:07:00,379 --> 00:07:01,463
[scoffs]

109
00:07:01,547 --> 00:07:03,257
Miss Bennett, I presume?

110
00:07:03,340 --> 00:07:04,758
Hello.

111
00:07:04,842 --> 00:07:06,844
Weren't we supposed to meet at the...

112
00:07:06,927 --> 00:07:08,846
You're not exactly hard to follow.

113
00:07:10,097 --> 00:07:11,974
So, how did you hear about me?

114
00:07:12,057 --> 00:07:14,560
- Paulo Pasolini, Blackwater.
- Mm-hmm.

115
00:07:14,643 --> 00:07:16,019
Does he have a message for me?

116
00:07:16,103 --> 00:07:20,107
Yes. He said to say,
"Felix isn't himself today."

117
00:07:20,190 --> 00:07:22,109
Oh. Okay, I'm listening.

118
00:07:22,192 --> 00:07:24,444
Is that some spy stuff?
'Cause that is fantastic.

119
00:07:26,613 --> 00:07:28,532
What is it that you do at ParkerGenix?

120
00:07:28,615 --> 00:07:33,245
My company is developing a new medication
and we have certain... needs.

121
00:07:33,328 --> 00:07:36,623
Uh-huh. That sounds vague.
And what does that mean in this case?

122
00:07:36,707 --> 00:07:40,085
They say you're good at procuring things
and not asking a lot of questions.

123
00:07:40,169 --> 00:07:41,962
[chuckles] Do they?

124
00:07:42,045 --> 00:07:46,466
They also say you're untroubled
by possible legal or ethical implications.

125
00:07:47,718 --> 00:07:49,678
Well, they need to shut up.

126
00:07:49,761 --> 00:07:52,723
Well, take it as a compliment.
Means you get the job done.

127
00:07:53,974 --> 00:07:55,976
I lost a friend on my last job,

128
00:07:56,059 --> 00:07:59,021
- so I am not up for procurement.
- Well, you don't know what it is yet.

129
00:07:59,104 --> 00:08:01,815
Yes, but you work for
a pharmaceutical company, right? So...

130
00:08:02,316 --> 00:08:04,985
You called me.
I'm spec ops, so it's covert.

131
00:08:05,944 --> 00:08:07,988
I'm guessing info theft.

132
00:08:08,071 --> 00:08:12,910
I'm sorry, I do retrieval
and extractions, not heists.

133
00:08:12,993 --> 00:08:14,536
It's dinosaurs.

134
00:08:14,620 --> 00:08:17,247
They may be through with us,
but we're not through with them.

135
00:08:17,331 --> 00:08:21,001
Well, they are all...
They're all dead or dying, so...

136
00:08:21,084 --> 00:08:23,754
Except for on a few islands
around the equator.

137
00:08:24,338 --> 00:08:27,257
Yes, and those are no-go zones
for a reason.

138
00:08:27,341 --> 00:08:29,176
You go there, you die.

139
00:08:29,259 --> 00:08:31,637
Which is where you come in.

140
00:08:31,720 --> 00:08:33,764
Oh. [chuckles]

141
00:08:33,847 --> 00:08:34,848
No, thanks.

142
00:08:37,351 --> 00:08:38,685
- Ms. Bennett!
- [scoffs]

143
00:08:38,769 --> 00:08:42,147
Did you know that 20% of the world's
population will die of heart disease?

144
00:08:42,231 --> 00:08:44,608
That's one in every five people
on the planet.

145
00:08:44,691 --> 00:08:46,860
Now, just imagine.
Take somebody you know, anybody,

146
00:08:46,944 --> 00:08:49,464
and imagine that you could be the one
to give them an extra 20 years of life.

147
00:08:49,488 --> 00:08:51,007
That's very touching. It's not my field.

148
00:08:51,031 --> 00:08:52,950
And we have very deep pockets.

149
00:09:00,874 --> 00:09:01,917
How deep?

150
00:09:03,043 --> 00:09:04,294
For you, extremely.

151
00:09:05,295 --> 00:09:06,797
Well...

152
00:09:07,422 --> 00:09:08,674
I'm gonna need a number.

153
00:09:09,174 --> 00:09:10,926
Ten, six zeros.

154
00:09:12,678 --> 00:09:14,346
Including the one that's in the ten?

155
00:09:14,429 --> 00:09:15,430
No.

156
00:09:16,974 --> 00:09:18,350
It's a fortune, Ms. Bennett.

157
00:09:19,393 --> 00:09:20,453
The kind of money that would mean

158
00:09:20,477 --> 00:09:22,729
you'd never have to put your life
on the line ever again.

159
00:09:23,397 --> 00:09:24,982
[chuckling]

160
00:09:25,065 --> 00:09:26,358
All right, what's the hitch?

161
00:09:26,984 --> 00:09:29,569
Uh... [clears throat]
Possible criminal charges.

162
00:09:30,195 --> 00:09:33,949
We are going to a place where
every government on the planet

163
00:09:34,032 --> 00:09:35,617
has outlawed travel.

164
00:09:37,619 --> 00:09:39,371
Well, we better not tell anyone, then.

165
00:09:42,708 --> 00:09:44,876
Yeah, that might be a problem.

166
00:09:44,960 --> 00:09:48,630
He doesn't know it yet,
but we're bringing a civilian.

167
00:09:49,298 --> 00:09:51,925
[dramatic orchestral music playing]

168
00:09:52,009 --> 00:09:53,319
- [music continues]
- [dinosaur grunts]

169
00:09:53,343 --> 00:09:57,222
[narrator] <i>The Neo-Jurassic Age</i>
<i>has proven lethal to Dinosauria.</i>

170
00:09:58,181 --> 00:10:00,392
<i>Though some species can survive</i>

171
00:10:00,475 --> 00:10:04,229
<i>in a few isolated oxygen-rich
tropical microclimates</i>

172
00:10:04,313 --> 00:10:06,523
<i>clustered in equatorial regions,</i>

173
00:10:06,606 --> 00:10:11,028
<i>where interactions with humans
and other incompatible modern species...</i>

174
00:10:18,535 --> 00:10:20,579
[worker] Banner coming down.

175
00:10:22,205 --> 00:10:24,875
- Okay, so this is the left metacarpal?
- Yeah.

176
00:10:28,503 --> 00:10:31,214
Ah. Good morning, Doctor.

177
00:10:31,298 --> 00:10:33,216
Sorry to drop in unannounced.

178
00:10:33,300 --> 00:10:34,468
Can we have a word?

179
00:10:34,551 --> 00:10:37,429
Sorry, it's a hell of a day here.
They're closing us down.

180
00:10:37,512 --> 00:10:39,431
Dr. Henry Loomis, Zora Bennett,

181
00:10:39,514 --> 00:10:41,099
- our mission specialist.
- Hello.

182
00:10:41,183 --> 00:10:42,183
Sorry, what mission?

183
00:10:44,019 --> 00:10:47,856
Uh, guys, why don't you just take
a break for a minute, okay?

184
00:10:47,939 --> 00:10:49,358
[chuckles]

185
00:10:49,441 --> 00:10:50,442
Thanks, guys.

186
00:10:50,525 --> 00:10:52,402
Dr. Loomis has been advising ParkerGenix

187
00:10:52,486 --> 00:10:54,696
for the past six months
on paleo-coronary health.

188
00:10:54,780 --> 00:10:56,907
We're developing a new drug, Paleodioxin,

189
00:10:56,990 --> 00:10:59,117
derived from the largest
dinosaur species on record.

190
00:10:59,701 --> 00:11:02,662
Computer modeling suggests
it could forestall coronary disease

191
00:11:02,746 --> 00:11:04,456
by an average of two decades.

192
00:11:04,539 --> 00:11:06,434
Just think what that might have meant
for your mother.

193
00:11:06,458 --> 00:11:08,335
- I'm so sorry for your loss.
- [chuckles]

194
00:11:08,418 --> 00:11:11,421
Okay, anybody can read a file.
If you get personal, though, I'm out.

195
00:11:11,922 --> 00:11:13,090
I'm sorry for overstepping.

196
00:11:13,673 --> 00:11:17,427
This research is crucial.
We just can't get it to trial.

197
00:11:17,511 --> 00:11:21,848
We're unable to synthesize the DNA,
so we need samples from living dinosaurs.

198
00:11:21,932 --> 00:11:24,935
And the species must be a true colossus.

199
00:11:25,018 --> 00:11:27,187
It's the biggest animals
across three categories:

200
00:11:27,270 --> 00:11:29,564
seagoing, land based, and avian.

201
00:11:30,107 --> 00:11:31,817
Why the biggest?

202
00:11:31,900 --> 00:11:33,777
It's their hearts.

203
00:11:33,860 --> 00:11:36,696
The bigger they were,
the bigger their coronary muscles.

204
00:11:36,780 --> 00:11:38,407
And they lived the longest,

205
00:11:38,490 --> 00:11:41,785
over a hundred years
for some Cretaceous-era sauropods.

206
00:11:41,868 --> 00:11:45,455
Which means, the three
dinosaurs they need are...

207
00:11:46,873 --> 00:11:48,750
Mosasaurus,

208
00:11:48,834 --> 00:11:51,169
which lives in the ocean.

209
00:11:52,587 --> 00:11:54,965
And Titanosaurus.

210
00:11:55,465 --> 00:11:57,342
You'll find them on land.

211
00:11:59,553 --> 00:12:01,012
And in the sky,

212
00:12:01,930 --> 00:12:05,100
Quetzalcoatlus.
It's the largest pterosaur.

213
00:12:06,101 --> 00:12:08,687
Each of them had an average lifespan
of well over a century,

214
00:12:08,770 --> 00:12:10,605
- even before DNA refinement.
- [Zora] Mmm.

215
00:12:10,689 --> 00:12:12,357
A drug that could prevent heart disease

216
00:12:12,441 --> 00:12:14,901
would be the biggest medical breakthrough
in centuries.

217
00:12:14,985 --> 00:12:18,155
Yeah, and I'm sure
it'd be worth billions, right?

218
00:12:19,281 --> 00:12:20,282
Trillions.

219
00:12:20,824 --> 00:12:23,243
The only way for our scientists
to complete the strands now

220
00:12:23,326 --> 00:12:26,746
is with blood and tissue samples
from living dinosaurs.

221
00:12:28,206 --> 00:12:31,751
Wait. You wanna take blood samples
from these animals while they're alive?

222
00:12:31,835 --> 00:12:35,046
Hemoglobin deoxygenates
within seconds postmortem.

223
00:12:35,797 --> 00:12:38,550
Lividity and hypostasis
follow immediately.

224
00:12:39,342 --> 00:12:44,598
Fortunately for us, all these species
exist in one isolated location.

225
00:12:44,681 --> 00:12:47,601
We're putting together a team,
and we'd like to leave immediately.

226
00:12:48,185 --> 00:12:51,730
We have competitors, and Mr. Parker
does not tolerate second place.

227
00:12:52,230 --> 00:12:53,398
Can you be ready tomorrow?

228
00:12:54,274 --> 00:12:55,275
Dr. Loomis?

229
00:12:57,986 --> 00:12:59,279
I thought I was just advising.

230
00:12:59,362 --> 00:13:00,906
You are. In person.

231
00:13:01,531 --> 00:13:04,868
We're not qualified to identify
the animals or predict behavior.

232
00:13:05,744 --> 00:13:06,745
Well... [chuckles]

233
00:13:08,747 --> 00:13:09,998
Why don't you send an army?

234
00:13:11,166 --> 00:13:13,585
- You're a rich company.
- Confidentiality.

235
00:13:14,211 --> 00:13:16,254
The fewer people know about this,
the better.

236
00:13:16,838 --> 00:13:20,258
Well, I can guarantee your safety.
I mean, you know, more or less.

237
00:13:20,342 --> 00:13:22,302
No, no, no. It's not about
my safety. It's just...

238
00:13:22,594 --> 00:13:24,179
- "More or less?"
- Yeah.

239
00:13:28,600 --> 00:13:31,686
[quietly] What you're proposing
is really illegal.

240
00:13:32,938 --> 00:13:34,523
Did you tell him about the zeros?

241
00:13:34,606 --> 00:13:37,086
- That's not gonna work with him.
- What are you talking about?

242
00:13:37,484 --> 00:13:38,485
[scoffs]

243
00:13:40,028 --> 00:13:41,905
You ever seen a dinosaur in the wild?

244
00:13:43,448 --> 00:13:45,408
I don't mean in a theme park
when you were a kid

245
00:13:45,492 --> 00:13:49,120
or watching some poor creature
wander around town, lost.

246
00:13:49,621 --> 00:13:51,748
I mean in its natural habitat.

247
00:13:53,375 --> 00:13:55,710
'Cause until you've done that,

248
00:13:55,794 --> 00:13:57,462
you're just a guy who visits zoos.

249
00:14:09,015 --> 00:14:10,016
[sighs]

250
00:14:13,103 --> 00:14:15,480
We sold a dozen tickets all last week.

251
00:14:18,358 --> 00:14:21,278
Five years ago,
you'd have to wait in line for hours,

252
00:14:21,361 --> 00:14:22,612
if you could even get in.

253
00:14:24,447 --> 00:14:26,408
[sighs]

254
00:14:26,491 --> 00:14:28,535
Nobody cares about these animals anymore.

255
00:14:31,121 --> 00:14:32,122
They deserve better.

256
00:14:33,873 --> 00:14:35,208
I just... I need a second.

257
00:14:36,418 --> 00:14:39,379
No, yeah. Take all the time that you need.

258
00:14:40,297 --> 00:14:42,465
Take all the minutes. Not like...

259
00:14:42,549 --> 00:14:44,301
[Krebs sighs]

260
00:14:44,384 --> 00:14:48,680
...your entire life has been,
you know, leading up to this moment.

261
00:14:48,763 --> 00:14:52,100
- [Krebs clears throat]
- [mint crunching]

262
00:14:53,226 --> 00:14:56,146
[crunching continues]

263
00:14:57,772 --> 00:15:00,984
[crunching continues]

264
00:15:04,904 --> 00:15:08,241
[crunching continues]

265
00:15:08,325 --> 00:15:10,243
- You ready?
- Yeah.

266
00:15:11,411 --> 00:15:14,414
[sweeping music playing]

267
00:15:28,762 --> 00:15:30,430
[excited chattering]

268
00:15:30,513 --> 00:15:32,557
[cheering]

269
00:15:39,105 --> 00:15:40,940
[sighs]

270
00:15:42,776 --> 00:15:44,986
["Movin' on Up" playing]

271
00:15:45,070 --> 00:15:47,322
[lively chattering in native language]

272
00:15:47,405 --> 00:15:48,406
[groans]

273
00:15:54,329 --> 00:15:56,539
[cheering]

274
00:15:57,165 --> 00:16:00,043
- Three in a row! [laughs]
- [excited chattering]

275
00:16:00,126 --> 00:16:01,336
[Krebs] Captain Kincaid.

276
00:16:03,880 --> 00:16:06,549
Martin Krebs. We spoke on the phone.

277
00:16:08,259 --> 00:16:11,054
Yeah, Krebs, look, I'm sorry.

278
00:16:11,971 --> 00:16:13,932
I thought it over, and I can't do it.

279
00:16:15,308 --> 00:16:18,812
I'd like to, just as a favor
to Zora, but I can't.

280
00:16:18,895 --> 00:16:20,438
[laughs]

281
00:16:20,522 --> 00:16:21,523
What?

282
00:16:22,524 --> 00:16:24,567
We already agreed on the phone.

283
00:16:26,236 --> 00:16:27,487
You named your price.

284
00:16:27,570 --> 00:16:30,490
It's not that. I just...
It's a little too risky for my boat.

285
00:16:32,283 --> 00:16:35,120
She's not paid off yet,
so I imagine you understand.

286
00:16:35,203 --> 00:16:37,038
No hard feelings, Z.

287
00:16:37,122 --> 00:16:39,708
- Hey. Two double rums. <i>Merci.</i>
- [chirping]

288
00:16:39,791 --> 00:16:42,502
What the hell is this? He's your guy.

289
00:16:42,585 --> 00:16:45,088
Yeah. We spent ten years
in MARSOC together.

290
00:16:46,214 --> 00:16:47,817
Duncan's the best,
otherwise I wouldn't be here.

291
00:16:47,841 --> 00:16:49,551
Best at what? Drinking?

292
00:16:49,634 --> 00:16:53,263
- Fabrice, not the guns, okay?
- All right. Okay.

293
00:16:53,346 --> 00:16:57,225
Moving things and people
in and out of places they shouldn't be.

294
00:16:57,892 --> 00:17:00,979
Trouble is, I really can't do it
this time, Z. I'm sorry.

295
00:17:01,062 --> 00:17:02,814
And I'm afraid that's final.

296
00:17:02,897 --> 00:17:04,232
What do you mean?

297
00:17:04,315 --> 00:17:06,609
That's a hell of a setback, Duncan.
You promised me.

298
00:17:07,652 --> 00:17:08,652
But we're screwed now.

299
00:17:11,239 --> 00:17:12,866
I'm sorry.

300
00:17:12,949 --> 00:17:14,909
I hate to see this all fall apart on you.

301
00:17:15,994 --> 00:17:17,287
[sighs]

302
00:17:18,538 --> 00:17:19,539
I'll tell you what.

303
00:17:20,707 --> 00:17:23,126
Why don't you make some
sort of good faith gesture?

304
00:17:23,209 --> 00:17:26,379
Like, maybe, you know,
I'm just spitballing here,

305
00:17:26,463 --> 00:17:30,300
but maybe you could double the number
that you and I spoke about,

306
00:17:30,383 --> 00:17:32,969
and then I can take care of Duncan
and his crew out of my end.

307
00:17:33,887 --> 00:17:34,971
[Krebs] Oh, I see.

308
00:17:35,054 --> 00:17:37,140
[chuckles] That's very nice.

309
00:17:37,223 --> 00:17:39,934
You guys cook this up
on the phone or just now?

310
00:17:40,018 --> 00:17:41,352
I'm just trying to help you out.

311
00:17:41,436 --> 00:17:43,813
I don't have to sit here and be insulted.

312
00:17:43,897 --> 00:17:45,899
For God sakes, fine. Agreed.

313
00:17:45,982 --> 00:17:47,025
Great.

314
00:17:47,108 --> 00:17:47,942
Great. [sighs]

315
00:17:48,026 --> 00:17:50,153
We'll loop up toward Barbados

316
00:17:51,446 --> 00:17:55,241
to avoid government patrols,
but there aren't many anyway.

317
00:17:57,535 --> 00:17:58,703
Why's that?

318
00:18:00,205 --> 00:18:02,540
[laughing]

319
00:18:02,624 --> 00:18:05,001
No one's dumb enough
to go where we're going.

320
00:18:05,084 --> 00:18:07,378
[kids chattering, laughing]

321
00:18:07,462 --> 00:18:08,505
Hey!

322
00:18:09,005 --> 00:18:11,174
Hey! Come here! Oh!

323
00:18:11,257 --> 00:18:12,300
[dinosaur grunting on TV]

324
00:18:12,383 --> 00:18:13,384
[laughing]

325
00:18:13,468 --> 00:18:14,844
- Hey. Over here.
- Hey.

326
00:18:14,928 --> 00:18:16,304
- Hey, Z.
- Hi.

327
00:18:16,971 --> 00:18:18,223
- Hey. How you been?
- Good.

328
00:18:18,306 --> 00:18:21,810
Bobby Atwater.
He'll be helping with team security.

329
00:18:21,893 --> 00:18:23,228
Security for?

330
00:18:24,270 --> 00:18:25,980
Make sure you don't end up
in one of these.

331
00:18:31,820 --> 00:18:32,946
A live one.

332
00:18:33,863 --> 00:18:35,406
Is there anything else?

333
00:18:36,074 --> 00:18:38,743
Yeah, listen, I'm not gonna
be able to do this, you know?

334
00:18:38,827 --> 00:18:40,554
I would love to
as a favor for you, Z, but...

335
00:18:40,578 --> 00:18:42,705
- Oh, no, we ran it already.
- All right, cool.

336
00:18:44,874 --> 00:18:46,084
So, when we leaving?

337
00:18:46,626 --> 00:18:49,254
[sweeping music playing]

338
00:18:59,055 --> 00:19:00,473
- [boat mate] Hey, LeClerc.
- What?

339
00:19:00,557 --> 00:19:02,660
Did you see how much Kincaid's
paying us for this one?

340
00:19:02,684 --> 00:19:05,478
- [laughing] Oh, yeah.
- [laughs]

341
00:19:05,562 --> 00:19:07,272
- We're in trouble.
- Hell, yeah.

342
00:19:07,355 --> 00:19:09,148
[radio beeps]

343
00:19:09,232 --> 00:19:11,818
[static, distorted chatter over radio]

344
00:19:14,112 --> 00:19:15,697
[Krebs] Well, the good news is,

345
00:19:15,780 --> 00:19:18,032
the Mosasaur was tagged in captivity,

346
00:19:18,116 --> 00:19:20,952
and InGen continued
to track it after its escape.

347
00:19:21,035 --> 00:19:22,847
We bought their data
when they entered chapter 11.

348
00:19:22,871 --> 00:19:25,039
So, now, we track it.

349
00:19:25,123 --> 00:19:28,293
Nobody else seems to know much about it,

350
00:19:28,376 --> 00:19:30,336
but that's it right there.

351
00:19:30,420 --> 00:19:33,798
So first sample in 150 miles or so.

352
00:19:33,882 --> 00:19:35,216
Very close to the equator.

353
00:19:36,509 --> 00:19:39,846
But why near the equator?
Why don't they survive anywhere else?

354
00:19:40,930 --> 00:19:43,850
The fitness landscape of the Earth
no longer suits them.

355
00:19:44,726 --> 00:19:46,227
The air is different.

356
00:19:47,020 --> 00:19:50,148
Uh, the solar radiation is different,
the insects, vegetation.

357
00:19:50,231 --> 00:19:51,649
Everything is different.

358
00:19:51,733 --> 00:19:55,778
But close to the equator, it's nearly
the climate of 60 million years ago.

359
00:19:55,862 --> 00:19:57,572
It's warmer and rich with oxygen.

360
00:19:57,655 --> 00:20:01,743
And the Mosasaur generally
circles the island we're headed to.

361
00:20:01,826 --> 00:20:06,122
There's a complex there
that was R and D for the original park.

362
00:20:07,248 --> 00:20:11,628
It was abandoned after an accident.

363
00:20:13,755 --> 00:20:16,049
We're gonna wanna get in,
get our three samples,

364
00:20:16,132 --> 00:20:18,343
and get out as quickly as possible.

365
00:20:19,093 --> 00:20:21,888
Because even though two dozen species
have survived there, alone,

366
00:20:21,971 --> 00:20:23,973
for almost 20 years,

367
00:20:25,224 --> 00:20:28,937
make no mistake, this is by no means
a controlled environment.

368
00:20:29,020 --> 00:20:31,648
[Atwater chuckles]
Yeah, I think we'll be okay.

369
00:20:32,815 --> 00:20:33,816
What are those?

370
00:20:34,525 --> 00:20:35,526
[Atwater] Neurotoxins.

371
00:20:36,611 --> 00:20:37,737
[Zora] How fast?

372
00:20:37,820 --> 00:20:40,531
[Atwater] Paralysis
within a couple of seconds.

373
00:20:40,615 --> 00:20:41,699
And then what?

374
00:20:44,202 --> 00:20:45,411
And then you survive.

375
00:20:52,168 --> 00:20:56,172
Is this the part where you, uh, tell me
it's a crime to kill a dinosaur, Doctor?

376
00:20:56,255 --> 00:20:59,133
No, this is where I tell you
it's a sin to kill a dinosaur.

377
00:21:00,468 --> 00:21:02,470
[chuckling]

378
00:21:03,262 --> 00:21:07,475
And so, uh, what if it's us or them, hmm?

379
00:21:08,017 --> 00:21:10,687
Then we've put ourselves in a place
where we don't belong.

380
00:21:11,229 --> 00:21:12,397
[scoffs]

381
00:21:12,480 --> 00:21:15,525
Hmm. That's kind of
our specialty, Dr. Loomis.

382
00:21:17,735 --> 00:21:19,380
We have no intention
of harming the animals.

383
00:21:19,404 --> 00:21:22,740
Let's just get our samples and go home.

384
00:21:22,824 --> 00:21:24,867
Gonna lose the light
before we make contact.

385
00:21:24,951 --> 00:21:26,703
We'll make a run
first thing in the morning.

386
00:21:27,704 --> 00:21:29,122
How does extraction work?

387
00:21:30,206 --> 00:21:32,000
[Loomis] These darts
collect blood samples.

388
00:21:32,083 --> 00:21:33,543
You get one shot with each.

389
00:21:33,626 --> 00:21:37,714
The Mosa has four inches of hide, so you
must be within ten meters to penetrate.

390
00:21:40,299 --> 00:21:42,552
And it self-ejects
when it reaches capacity.

391
00:21:42,635 --> 00:21:44,095
[dart beeping]

392
00:21:44,178 --> 00:21:47,432
It should fire a couple of hundred feet
in the air, like a model rocket.

393
00:21:51,102 --> 00:21:54,689
On the day, a parachute will open,
and it'll float back down to us.

394
00:21:55,273 --> 00:21:56,315
I'll take the shot.

395
00:21:56,399 --> 00:22:00,278
Uh, remote biopsy darting is pretty basic.
I'm sure I can handle it.

396
00:22:01,738 --> 00:22:02,780
Oh.

397
00:22:02,864 --> 00:22:04,073
[laughing]

398
00:22:05,450 --> 00:22:06,659
Let me show you something.

399
00:22:11,706 --> 00:22:13,875
- You feel that spray?
- Yeah.

400
00:22:13,958 --> 00:22:16,919
Now imagine the boat rocking
at 15 to 20 degrees,

401
00:22:17,003 --> 00:22:19,756
we're moving at 30 knots,
you got sweat running in your eyes,

402
00:22:19,839 --> 00:22:22,279
and you're close enough
to look that thing right in the teeth.

403
00:22:23,176 --> 00:22:24,343
You got this.

404
00:22:31,225 --> 00:22:32,435
Uh, please, be my guest.

405
00:22:32,518 --> 00:22:34,437
Oh, really? If you insist.

406
00:22:38,691 --> 00:22:40,485
[chuckling]

407
00:22:41,444 --> 00:22:43,529
[chuckling continues] I can't take it.

408
00:22:44,113 --> 00:22:45,823
[radio beeping]

409
00:22:45,907 --> 00:22:48,785
- It's been fun working with you, Z.
- What?

410
00:22:48,868 --> 00:22:50,953
But I think we just found
our new team leader.

411
00:22:51,037 --> 00:22:52,455
[laughing]

412
00:22:52,538 --> 00:22:53,706
No offense.

413
00:22:54,457 --> 00:22:57,168
I mean, I don't know anybody
that could be offended by that.

414
00:22:57,251 --> 00:22:58,377
That's mean.

415
00:23:01,005 --> 00:23:02,006
Oh.

416
00:23:04,592 --> 00:23:06,135
I heard about Booker.

417
00:23:06,219 --> 00:23:09,180
[inhales deeply, sighs]

418
00:23:09,263 --> 00:23:10,389
Yeah.

419
00:23:10,473 --> 00:23:11,849
I'm so sorry.

420
00:23:13,059 --> 00:23:14,060
Yeah.

421
00:23:16,270 --> 00:23:17,688
What job were you on?

422
00:23:17,772 --> 00:23:22,693
[sighs] Um, just a simple
training mission in Yemen.

423
00:23:24,028 --> 00:23:26,656
Couldn't have been more basic.
Just a car bomb.

424
00:23:28,074 --> 00:23:29,450
Out of nowhere.

425
00:23:32,203 --> 00:23:33,454
Quick at least.

426
00:23:36,290 --> 00:23:37,625
Not really.

427
00:23:38,209 --> 00:23:39,752
Ah, shit.

428
00:23:42,046 --> 00:23:43,548
He didn't deserve that.

429
00:23:45,383 --> 00:23:46,384
No.

430
00:23:47,051 --> 00:23:48,052
[sighs]

431
00:23:50,304 --> 00:23:54,142
I had to tell his wife. It was brutal.

432
00:23:54,809 --> 00:23:57,019
- You can't keep this up forever, Z.
- No.

433
00:23:57,103 --> 00:23:58,354
This work.

434
00:23:59,814 --> 00:24:01,399
It breaks you eventually.

435
00:24:01,482 --> 00:24:03,002
What do you think I'm doing down here?

436
00:24:03,818 --> 00:24:07,238
I'm just trying to, you know,
buy my life back.

437
00:24:07,321 --> 00:24:09,615
Really? I thought you just missed me.

438
00:24:09,699 --> 00:24:11,325
- Well.
- [chuckles]

439
00:24:12,368 --> 00:24:14,620
- You know what I actually missed?
- Mmm.

440
00:24:15,913 --> 00:24:17,290
My own mother's funeral.

441
00:24:18,374 --> 00:24:19,458
Oh, wow.

442
00:24:19,542 --> 00:24:20,877
Yeah.

443
00:24:20,960 --> 00:24:22,795
That's terrible, Zora.

444
00:24:22,879 --> 00:24:24,797
Yeah. Yeah.

445
00:24:24,881 --> 00:24:27,642
No, seriously. That's like the worst thing
I've ever heard anybody do.

446
00:24:27,717 --> 00:24:29,844
Thank you. That's so supportive of you.

447
00:24:29,927 --> 00:24:30,928
Oh.

448
00:24:31,762 --> 00:24:32,763
God.

449
00:24:40,104 --> 00:24:42,064
Please tell me
you worked it out with Amelia.

450
00:24:48,404 --> 00:24:49,447
Well.

451
00:24:52,700 --> 00:24:54,785
Whenever we looked at each other, we just...

452
00:24:57,538 --> 00:24:59,081
just saw our little boy.

453
00:25:02,293 --> 00:25:05,338
Easier for us both just to
go at it alone, you know?

454
00:25:15,348 --> 00:25:16,515
We are very pathetic.

455
00:25:18,643 --> 00:25:21,520
All right, what do you say we stay alive?

456
00:25:22,104 --> 00:25:24,190
And, uh, you know,

457
00:25:25,107 --> 00:25:26,317
get rich this time.

458
00:25:27,693 --> 00:25:30,071
Rich is nice, but it won't be enough.

459
00:25:31,447 --> 00:25:32,490
For sure.

460
00:25:32,573 --> 00:25:35,159
Do something that matters
while there's still time.

461
00:25:35,243 --> 00:25:36,244
Okay.

462
00:25:36,327 --> 00:25:38,120
Don't just brush that off. I mean it.

463
00:25:39,247 --> 00:25:40,665
Don't wait, Zora.

464
00:25:41,165 --> 00:25:42,625
Do not wait.

465
00:25:42,708 --> 00:25:43,709
[sighs]

466
00:25:46,754 --> 00:25:49,215
["Harmony Hall" playing]

467
00:25:56,472 --> 00:25:58,724
<i>♪ We took a vow in summertime ♪</i>

468
00:25:58,808 --> 00:26:02,228
<i>♪ Now we find ourselves
In late December... ♪</i>

469
00:26:05,773 --> 00:26:07,024
You're rationing those, honey?

470
00:26:08,484 --> 00:26:09,986
I'm enjoying them.

471
00:26:10,069 --> 00:26:13,364
[sighs, grunts] Honey,
it's a long way across the Atlantic.

472
00:26:13,447 --> 00:26:15,283
You're gonna miss 'em when you run out.

473
00:26:18,661 --> 00:26:21,080
Can I ask why you have
so many in your pocket?

474
00:26:22,999 --> 00:26:24,208
So he doesn't eat them.

475
00:26:25,001 --> 00:26:26,294
Ah, right.

476
00:26:27,169 --> 00:26:29,213
Hey, honey. Where's your boyfriend?

477
00:26:29,297 --> 00:26:30,840
It's his turn at the wheel.

478
00:26:30,923 --> 00:26:33,884
- Is he coming or what?
- I told him.

479
00:26:33,968 --> 00:26:35,594
He's just getting dressed.

480
00:26:36,887 --> 00:26:37,888
Okay.

481
00:26:38,723 --> 00:26:41,559
[sighs] Okay. Do you wanna practice?

482
00:26:42,226 --> 00:26:43,394
All right.

483
00:26:43,477 --> 00:26:46,314
Remember, okay,
it's just like Dad showed you.

484
00:26:46,397 --> 00:26:48,649
Under, under, around.

485
00:26:48,733 --> 00:26:50,401
You guys ever wish you had a boring dad?

486
00:26:50,484 --> 00:26:52,028
- We do.
- Yeah.

487
00:26:52,111 --> 00:26:53,279
[chuckling]

488
00:26:54,196 --> 00:26:55,197
[boyfriend] Ah.

489
00:26:55,948 --> 00:26:57,158
Finally.

490
00:27:01,746 --> 00:27:05,249
- Good evening, Xavier.
- Ah, what's up?

491
00:27:06,042 --> 00:27:07,335
Six o'clock.

492
00:27:07,418 --> 00:27:08,753
[laughs] I know.

493
00:27:10,963 --> 00:27:12,923
I was out cold.

494
00:27:13,591 --> 00:27:15,009
It's your watch.

495
00:27:15,092 --> 00:27:16,761
I slept like a baby.

496
00:27:20,056 --> 00:27:21,807
You-You've got the wheel?

497
00:27:23,768 --> 00:27:26,937
Man, I am so tired.

498
00:27:27,021 --> 00:27:29,857
I'm gonna be honest.
I'd probably conk out at the wheel.

499
00:27:29,940 --> 00:27:31,776
I'll take his shift. It's fine.

500
00:27:31,859 --> 00:27:34,070
No, no, no, honey,
we've been through this.

501
00:27:34,153 --> 00:27:35,905
You need sleep. I need sleep.

502
00:27:35,988 --> 00:27:38,240
It's your shift, Xavier. Come on, man.

503
00:27:38,949 --> 00:27:42,119
Reuben, can't we just anchor, for once?

504
00:27:42,953 --> 00:27:44,580
We can use the anchor.

505
00:27:44,663 --> 00:27:46,290
We haven't done that yet.

506
00:27:46,374 --> 00:27:48,709
We only carry 50 meters of anchor chain.

507
00:27:49,293 --> 00:27:51,128
It's 2,000 meters to the bottom.

508
00:27:51,879 --> 00:27:53,047
[Xavier] Oh.

509
00:27:55,049 --> 00:27:57,486
Kinda sounds like somebody should've
thought about that before we left.

510
00:27:57,510 --> 00:27:59,112
Do you have any idea
the weight of two thou...

511
00:27:59,136 --> 00:28:01,889
I built this boat, okay, Xavier?
I know what I'm doing.

512
00:28:01,972 --> 00:28:03,766
So you're good? You're good on the wheel.

513
00:28:03,849 --> 00:28:06,352
You know what?
Just grab another hour. I'm fine.

514
00:28:06,435 --> 00:28:08,562
- Yes. Perfect. You're the best.
- I got it. I'm fine.

515
00:28:08,646 --> 00:28:11,232
Fist bump? Give me some.

516
00:28:11,315 --> 00:28:12,566
Never mind. [grunts]

517
00:28:13,234 --> 00:28:15,569
Oh, you got licorice, dude?

518
00:28:15,653 --> 00:28:16,654
I love this stuff.

519
00:28:28,749 --> 00:28:31,585
I didn't say a word. Not-Not one word.

520
00:28:32,336 --> 00:28:33,337
Good.

521
00:28:34,130 --> 00:28:35,339
So don't.

522
00:28:37,883 --> 00:28:39,593
What... Did he even bring a shirt?

523
00:28:39,677 --> 00:28:43,472
Okay, clearly, you don't see
the side of him that I see.

524
00:28:44,056 --> 00:28:45,057
The naked side.

525
00:28:45,141 --> 00:28:46,559
Hey! [scoffs]

526
00:28:47,393 --> 00:28:50,980
I mean, I... I guess I'd hoped
it would just be the three of us.

527
00:28:51,730 --> 00:28:53,816
You know, one last hurrah.

528
00:28:54,525 --> 00:28:57,319
I'm going to NYU, not Mongolia.

529
00:28:58,696 --> 00:29:00,030
I know. You're right.

530
00:29:06,328 --> 00:29:07,496
What are those?

531
00:29:10,124 --> 00:29:11,125
Where?

532
00:29:17,131 --> 00:29:19,633
One o'clock. Moving east.

533
00:29:19,717 --> 00:29:22,386
- Dolphins?
- No.

534
00:29:22,470 --> 00:29:24,013
No fins.

535
00:29:24,972 --> 00:29:26,182
Sails.

536
00:29:30,603 --> 00:29:32,855
- [boat rumbling]
- [grunting]

537
00:29:35,191 --> 00:29:37,359
Bella, grab the wheel.

538
00:29:37,443 --> 00:29:39,361
[dramatic music playing]

539
00:29:43,449 --> 00:29:45,868
[grunts, pants]

540
00:29:46,994 --> 00:29:48,496
- Left, left, left.
- [grunts]

541
00:29:52,124 --> 00:29:54,043
[grunting]

542
00:29:56,545 --> 00:29:57,755
Something big sideswiped us.

543
00:29:57,838 --> 00:29:58,923
Sailfish?

544
00:29:59,715 --> 00:30:00,799
Bigger.

545
00:30:00,883 --> 00:30:03,344
Is it the you-know-what?

546
00:30:04,011 --> 00:30:07,389
No, honey, it's not a you-know-what.
There's barely any left.

547
00:30:07,473 --> 00:30:10,559
[Isabella] I hate those things.
I wish they never came back.

548
00:30:11,727 --> 00:30:13,812
I think we should just head ba...
[screaming]

549
00:30:13,896 --> 00:30:17,191
- [dinosaur roaring]
- [all shouting]

550
00:30:17,274 --> 00:30:19,109
[Isabella screaming]

551
00:30:21,862 --> 00:30:24,323
[screaming]

552
00:30:24,406 --> 00:30:25,406
Hold on!

553
00:30:32,206 --> 00:30:33,207
Dad!

554
00:30:33,707 --> 00:30:34,708
- Dad!
- Teresa!

555
00:30:34,792 --> 00:30:36,460
Xavier's inside.

556
00:30:36,544 --> 00:30:38,629
I'll get him. Get to the top!
Get to the top!

557
00:30:41,298 --> 00:30:42,466
[Teresa] Swim!

558
00:30:42,550 --> 00:30:44,134
Xavier!

559
00:30:44,218 --> 00:30:46,898
- It's not opening! Reuben, it won't open.
- Let it fill with water.

560
00:30:46,929 --> 00:30:48,931
Let it fill, then you can open the door.

561
00:30:49,014 --> 00:30:51,058
Get the radio! In the red bag!

562
00:30:51,141 --> 00:30:52,560
The red bag! The emergency bag!

563
00:30:52,643 --> 00:30:54,019
- Get up!
- Okay.

564
00:30:57,898 --> 00:30:59,233
Go!

565
00:30:59,316 --> 00:31:01,026
- Where is it? I can't find it!
- Get it!

566
00:31:01,110 --> 00:31:03,862
It's in the bag, the emergency bag.
The emergency radio.

567
00:31:03,946 --> 00:31:04,947
Okay.

568
00:31:07,908 --> 00:31:09,451
Dad!

569
00:31:09,535 --> 00:31:10,995
[Teresa] Xavier!

570
00:31:11,078 --> 00:31:13,455
Hey! Just get out. [inhales deeply]

571
00:31:14,957 --> 00:31:16,458
[inhales deeply]

572
00:31:33,684 --> 00:31:34,727
Teresa!

573
00:31:34,810 --> 00:31:35,894
Bella! Bella, hold on.

574
00:31:41,692 --> 00:31:44,111
[dinosaur bellowing]

575
00:31:47,031 --> 00:31:49,033
- [Teresa] Xavier!
- [Isabella] Xavier!

576
00:31:50,659 --> 00:31:52,369
- Dad!
- Dad!

577
00:31:52,453 --> 00:31:53,871
Dad!

578
00:31:53,954 --> 00:31:56,582
- Xavier!
- Dad!

579
00:31:56,665 --> 00:31:59,043
- Dad!
- Honey!

580
00:31:59,877 --> 00:32:01,170
Dad!

581
00:32:01,253 --> 00:32:03,005
Where's Xavier?

582
00:32:03,088 --> 00:32:04,506
What happened?

583
00:32:04,590 --> 00:32:06,717
Dad, where is he?

584
00:32:06,800 --> 00:32:07,926
[Reuben] I don't know.

585
00:32:08,927 --> 00:32:11,180
I'll go back down. I'll keep looking.

586
00:32:11,263 --> 00:32:13,682
[Xavier gasping]

587
00:32:13,766 --> 00:32:15,643
Xavier! Xavier!

588
00:32:15,726 --> 00:32:18,062
[bellowing]

589
00:32:18,145 --> 00:32:19,355
Xavier!

590
00:32:19,438 --> 00:32:21,148
Xavier, swim!

591
00:32:21,231 --> 00:32:22,733
Xavier, hurry!

592
00:32:22,816 --> 00:32:24,985
- Swim! Xavier!
- Come on!

593
00:32:27,029 --> 00:32:28,614
[panting]

594
00:32:28,697 --> 00:32:30,383
- Come on! Come on! Come on!
- Swim, Xavier!

595
00:32:30,407 --> 00:32:31,784
Faster! Come on!

596
00:32:31,867 --> 00:32:34,370
- Hurry! Faster!
- Xavier! Come on!

597
00:32:35,913 --> 00:32:37,915
[bellowing]

598
00:32:37,998 --> 00:32:39,541
[grunting]

599
00:32:44,421 --> 00:32:45,422
Dad.

600
00:32:50,427 --> 00:32:51,470
[Xavier] I...

601
00:32:52,638 --> 00:32:53,806
I got the bag.

602
00:32:58,102 --> 00:33:01,647
[thunder rumbling]

603
00:33:02,648 --> 00:33:05,025
["This Girl" playing on stereo]

604
00:33:05,109 --> 00:33:06,360
<i>♪ Money rains... ♪</i>

605
00:33:06,443 --> 00:33:08,362
[chattering in French]

606
00:33:09,613 --> 00:33:12,491
<i>♪ But keep the change
'Cause I've got enough ♪</i>

607
00:33:12,574 --> 00:33:14,243
[both laughing]

608
00:33:14,326 --> 00:33:16,704
<i>- ♪ A little time... ♪</i>
- [song fades]

609
00:33:25,796 --> 00:33:27,256
[door opens]

610
00:33:28,090 --> 00:33:29,216
Am I interrupting?

611
00:33:30,134 --> 00:33:32,136
Oh. No.

612
00:33:32,803 --> 00:33:33,804
It's fine.

613
00:33:34,972 --> 00:33:36,557
I can't sleep. [chuckles]

614
00:33:36,640 --> 00:33:37,641
[chuckles]

615
00:33:37,725 --> 00:33:39,685
Well, they say if you can sleep
the night before,

616
00:33:39,768 --> 00:33:40,894
you should probably quit.

617
00:33:42,896 --> 00:33:44,496
You don't hear that much in museum work.

618
00:33:46,316 --> 00:33:47,317
You wanna sit down?

619
00:33:56,368 --> 00:33:57,995
Can I ask what was bothering you?

620
00:33:59,329 --> 00:34:00,539
Maybe a little PTSD.

621
00:34:00,622 --> 00:34:04,293
I probably should've taken
some more time off after my last job.

622
00:34:08,881 --> 00:34:09,882
What are you?

623
00:34:11,633 --> 00:34:13,177
Like a mercenary?

624
00:34:15,220 --> 00:34:17,055
Situational security and reaction.

625
00:34:21,393 --> 00:34:22,770
So a mercenary, then.

626
00:34:22,853 --> 00:34:23,937
[chuckles]

627
00:34:25,981 --> 00:34:28,901
Well, still, sounds way more exciting
than my life.

628
00:34:28,984 --> 00:34:30,736
Yeah? How exactly?

629
00:34:32,237 --> 00:34:34,239
Well, I have never been shot at.

630
00:34:36,825 --> 00:34:37,910
No?

631
00:34:37,993 --> 00:34:39,787
- Mm-mmm.
- Wow.

632
00:34:40,954 --> 00:34:41,955
Well.

633
00:34:43,457 --> 00:34:44,666
There's still time.

634
00:34:48,879 --> 00:34:50,255
Good night, Doctor.

635
00:34:52,674 --> 00:34:53,675
Good night.

636
00:35:14,530 --> 00:35:17,241
[seagulls calling in distance]

637
00:35:18,784 --> 00:35:19,785
[static crackling]

638
00:35:19,868 --> 00:35:22,830
[Reuben on radio] <i>Mayday. Mayday. Mayday.</i>
<i>This is the</i> Mariposa.

639
00:35:22,913 --> 00:35:25,290
<i>Last known position 24..</i>. [garbled speech]

640
00:35:25,374 --> 00:35:28,377
[garbled radio chatter continues]

641
00:35:28,460 --> 00:35:30,045
[snoring]

642
00:35:35,175 --> 00:35:36,510
[toy dinosaur roaring]

643
00:35:36,593 --> 00:35:38,053
[gasps]

644
00:35:39,221 --> 00:35:40,764
[in French]

645
00:35:40,848 --> 00:35:43,600
- What's our distance to target?
- [grunts] Ah.

646
00:35:44,393 --> 00:35:45,769
[in French]

647
00:35:47,479 --> 00:35:49,106
Just resting your eyes, huh?

648
00:35:49,940 --> 00:35:51,608
<i>Euh, ouais.</i>

649
00:35:51,692 --> 00:35:53,443
[in French]

650
00:35:54,736 --> 00:35:55,737
<i>Merci.</i>

651
00:35:55,821 --> 00:35:57,155
No French before breakfast.

652
00:35:59,616 --> 00:36:01,118
[garbled radio chatter continues]

653
00:36:01,201 --> 00:36:03,537
<i>Mayday. Mayday. Mayday.</i>
<i>This is the</i> Mariposa.

654
00:36:03,620 --> 00:36:04,872
<i>We are sinking.</i>

655
00:36:04,955 --> 00:36:09,376
<i>Last known position 24 degrees 33 north
and 74, 56 west.</i>

656
00:36:09,459 --> 00:36:10,502
<i>We are sinking.</i>

657
00:36:10,586 --> 00:36:13,426
[Zora] The signal was clear, right?
I mean, you sure you heard a mayday?

658
00:36:13,505 --> 00:36:14,506
Positive.

659
00:36:14,590 --> 00:36:15,650
But did you get a location?

660
00:36:15,674 --> 00:36:18,093
Down to ten square meters.
They sent a DSC.

661
00:36:18,969 --> 00:36:20,637
Twenty-eight miles away.

662
00:36:20,721 --> 00:36:25,475
Well, that's 28 miles, you know,
in the opposite direction.

663
00:36:25,976 --> 00:36:27,787
What? Bobby, what, do you want
to leave them? Come on.

664
00:36:27,811 --> 00:36:29,313
Nah, of course not.

665
00:36:29,396 --> 00:36:31,523
I'm saying this guy has a working radio.

666
00:36:31,607 --> 00:36:33,400
And, you know,
he can transmit his location.

667
00:36:33,483 --> 00:36:35,683
It's true. It's not as if
they're gonna freeze to death.

668
00:36:36,194 --> 00:36:38,947
I mean, I'm saying, like,
help is gonna be on its way.

669
00:36:39,031 --> 00:36:41,217
There's probably a coast guard ship
on its way right now.

670
00:36:41,241 --> 00:36:42,534
Two miles.

671
00:36:44,870 --> 00:36:47,706
Look, we are three minutes away
from this Mosasaur,

672
00:36:47,789 --> 00:36:49,791
which, may I remind you,
is what we came here for.

673
00:36:49,875 --> 00:36:53,378
The Mosasaur is tagged.
We found it once, we'll find it again.

674
00:36:53,462 --> 00:36:57,966
Maybe we can, but, Z, we're talking about
tens of millions of dollars here.

675
00:36:58,050 --> 00:37:00,802
And all they need to do is just
float around for a couple of hours.

676
00:37:00,886 --> 00:37:02,387
I've got visual.

677
00:37:02,471 --> 00:37:04,431
13 degrees off the port side.

678
00:37:06,975 --> 00:37:07,976
[Loomis] That's it.

679
00:37:09,519 --> 00:37:11,980
Okay, I have to agree with Mr., um...

680
00:37:12,064 --> 00:37:13,607
- Atwater.
- Atwater.

681
00:37:14,483 --> 00:37:16,944
- Right.
- The Mosasaur first.

682
00:37:17,027 --> 00:37:18,028
Right?

683
00:37:21,949 --> 00:37:23,992
LeClerc, new course.

684
00:37:24,076 --> 00:37:27,162
24, 33 north, 74, 56 west.

685
00:37:28,121 --> 00:37:29,122
Flank speed.

686
00:37:29,206 --> 00:37:30,207
[LeClerc] Aye aye.

687
00:37:30,832 --> 00:37:32,668
We're search and rescue now.

688
00:37:35,212 --> 00:37:36,421
It's my charter.

689
00:37:37,464 --> 00:37:39,132
It's my boat.

690
00:37:51,687 --> 00:37:54,064
[groans, sighs]

691
00:38:00,529 --> 00:38:03,115
[rummaging in bag]

692
00:38:03,198 --> 00:38:05,450
[hopeful music playing]

693
00:38:15,919 --> 00:38:17,504
[Xavier] Is that... Is that?

694
00:38:17,587 --> 00:38:18,982
- Oh, my God. Hey.
- [Teresa] Oh, my God.

695
00:38:19,006 --> 00:38:20,716
[Xavier] Hey. Hey!

696
00:38:20,799 --> 00:38:21,985
- Hey!
- [Teresa] Hey, over here!

697
00:38:22,009 --> 00:38:23,510
Over here!

698
00:38:29,975 --> 00:38:32,436
[Teresa] I don't know.
It was, like, the size of the boat.

699
00:38:32,519 --> 00:38:35,063
Bigger. 50 feet. 70, I don't know.

700
00:38:35,147 --> 00:38:38,275
It's a snakelike body,
with a large skull and a long snout?

701
00:38:38,358 --> 00:38:39,276
Yes, yes.

702
00:38:39,359 --> 00:38:40,902
- And limbs like paddles?
- Yes, yes.

703
00:38:40,986 --> 00:38:42,738
A long tail region with a downcurved end?

704
00:38:42,821 --> 00:38:44,841
Look, man, that's what it is.
What else could it be?

705
00:38:44,865 --> 00:38:47,025
What the hell you doing this far out
with a little kid?

706
00:38:47,659 --> 00:38:50,454
We're just making a crossing.
Barbados to Cape Town.

707
00:38:50,537 --> 00:38:51,997
We've done it before.

708
00:38:52,080 --> 00:38:53,373
Shouldn't they be in school?

709
00:38:55,584 --> 00:38:58,295
They are. It's summer break.
What is your problem?

710
00:38:58,378 --> 00:38:59,939
My problem is you should've known better.

711
00:38:59,963 --> 00:39:02,924
How? There are 50,000 boats
on this ocean right now.

712
00:39:03,008 --> 00:39:05,510
One was attacked by a Mosasaur.
What are the odds?

713
00:39:05,594 --> 00:39:08,764
Duncan, let it go, okay?
You know you're not pissed off at him.

714
00:39:08,847 --> 00:39:11,058
Okay? These kids are safe now.

715
00:39:12,059 --> 00:39:13,602
All right. I don't understand.

716
00:39:13,685 --> 00:39:16,646
Why would a Mosasaur attack
a 45-foot sailboat? It's not food.

717
00:39:16,730 --> 00:39:19,441
[Loomis] Maybe it thought
the boat was a rival,

718
00:39:19,524 --> 00:39:21,485
and it wanted to eliminate
any competition.

719
00:39:22,819 --> 00:39:23,987
How far off course are we?

720
00:39:24,071 --> 00:39:25,072
Twenty-eight miles.

721
00:39:25,155 --> 00:39:26,782
And how long to reacquire the target?

722
00:39:26,865 --> 00:39:29,326
Uh, two hours, maybe.

723
00:39:29,409 --> 00:39:31,387
Depends how fast it's moving
and in which direction.

724
00:39:31,411 --> 00:39:35,582
Well, what say we turn the boat around
and get the mission back on track.

725
00:39:35,665 --> 00:39:38,585
Wait, wait, wait. How fast what's moving?
What mission?

726
00:39:38,668 --> 00:39:41,004
So... [chuckles] About that. It's, uh...

727
00:39:41,088 --> 00:39:43,965
Okay. So, exactly
what are you guys doing here?

728
00:39:44,549 --> 00:39:48,428
Uh, we are gonna take a detour
and pick up a few items, and that's it.

729
00:39:49,554 --> 00:39:51,681
- What... What items?
- It's okay, honey.

730
00:39:51,765 --> 00:39:53,642
- It's okay.
- What is she talking about?

731
00:39:54,184 --> 00:39:55,393
Hey. That guy...

732
00:39:56,478 --> 00:39:57,478
Hi. Excuse me.

733
00:39:57,521 --> 00:39:58,521
...is creeping me out.

734
00:39:59,106 --> 00:40:00,816
- What'd I do?
- You're creeping me out.

735
00:40:01,483 --> 00:40:04,027
Can we get the tracking map up, please?

736
00:40:04,111 --> 00:40:05,737
All right, look. Look...

737
00:40:06,988 --> 00:40:09,407
we understand
you've got your own business, and...

738
00:40:09,491 --> 00:40:11,076
and, um...

739
00:40:11,159 --> 00:40:12,285
we're very grateful to you.

740
00:40:12,369 --> 00:40:14,764
You pulled us out of the water,
we're not going to forget that,

741
00:40:14,788 --> 00:40:17,332
- but can you just drop us off from...
- Is that it?

742
00:40:17,415 --> 00:40:19,175
- That would be great.
- [Nina] Flip this up.

743
00:40:19,668 --> 00:40:20,752
It's hard to tell.

744
00:40:20,836 --> 00:40:22,436
You can leave us at any convenient port.

745
00:40:23,171 --> 00:40:25,549
Please. Yeah?

746
00:40:26,550 --> 00:40:28,486
The software's ghosting.
It's doubling the signal.

747
00:40:28,510 --> 00:40:31,221
- Maybe it's just echoing off our boat.
- That's not an echo.

748
00:40:31,304 --> 00:40:32,365
- What?
- What is it you need to get?

749
00:40:32,389 --> 00:40:33,640
That's two signals.

750
00:40:33,723 --> 00:40:35,642
[Nina speaking French quietly]

751
00:40:35,725 --> 00:40:37,269
So we already found it?

752
00:40:37,352 --> 00:40:38,436
No, no.

753
00:40:39,688 --> 00:40:40,939
It found us.

754
00:40:41,022 --> 00:40:43,066
[alarm blaring]

755
00:40:43,150 --> 00:40:44,877
- Oh, no, no. Come on, guys.
- [Teresa] Okay...

756
00:40:44,901 --> 00:40:47,404
- [boat rumbling]
- [all gasping]

757
00:40:48,280 --> 00:40:50,341
- [Loomis] It's here.
- [Kincaid] Nina, tell me something.

758
00:40:50,365 --> 00:40:51,885
[Nina] Everything's online. It's fine.

759
00:40:51,950 --> 00:40:54,428
[Teresa] Okay, so we just got away from
it, and you guys have been chasing it?

760
00:40:54,452 --> 00:40:55,662
[Nina] Starboard. Yeah, yeah.

761
00:40:55,745 --> 00:40:58,123
Guys, listen. We're gonna be
a couple of minutes tops.

762
00:40:58,206 --> 00:41:00,750
Then we're all going to this island
like she said, okay?

763
00:41:00,834 --> 00:41:04,045
There's a village complex there
with geothermal energy that still works.

764
00:41:04,129 --> 00:41:05,731
You can wait there.
You'll be totally safe.

765
00:41:05,755 --> 00:41:07,108
- [Xavier] What?
- [Teresa] Oh, my God.

766
00:41:07,132 --> 00:41:09,467
- [Kincaid] There, starboard.
- [Krebs] Okay.

767
00:41:09,551 --> 00:41:10,594
[Zora] Let's go!

768
00:41:10,677 --> 00:41:12,846
- Take the wheel. I'll get a bearing.
- Aye, aye.

769
00:41:14,139 --> 00:41:16,141
[dramatic music playing]

770
00:41:21,021 --> 00:41:22,772
[bellowing]

771
00:41:22,856 --> 00:41:25,400
Ah! It's beautiful!

772
00:41:25,984 --> 00:41:27,527
- [Nina] Yes!
- Whoo!

773
00:41:28,153 --> 00:41:29,154
Let's go!

774
00:41:32,365 --> 00:41:35,744
[bellowing]

775
00:41:38,538 --> 00:41:39,539
We're losing her.

776
00:41:47,005 --> 00:41:49,841
Loomis, I need you
to secure me to the bow rail!

777
00:41:49,925 --> 00:41:50,926
[Loomis] Okay.

778
00:41:53,053 --> 00:41:54,054
[Zora] Make it tight.

779
00:41:54,804 --> 00:41:56,348
- Like this?
- Yeah.

780
00:41:57,807 --> 00:41:59,392
- The other side!
- Okay.

781
00:42:01,519 --> 00:42:04,189
Remember, don't fire
until it's within ten meters.

782
00:42:04,272 --> 00:42:05,273
[Zora] Got it.

783
00:42:12,239 --> 00:42:13,448
All right.

784
00:42:13,531 --> 00:42:15,533
Nina, I need command up here now.

785
00:42:15,617 --> 00:42:17,118
[console beeping]

786
00:42:17,202 --> 00:42:18,787
Comm is yours.

787
00:42:26,586 --> 00:42:29,506
[bellowing]

788
00:42:29,589 --> 00:42:31,424
Come on. Come on!

789
00:42:31,508 --> 00:42:33,927
We gotta be closer. Move it!

790
00:42:34,010 --> 00:42:35,845
Hey. Get up here and spot.

791
00:42:35,929 --> 00:42:37,097
[Nina on radio] <i>On my way.</i>

792
00:42:39,641 --> 00:42:40,767
20 meters.

793
00:42:44,437 --> 00:42:45,814
Almost in range!

794
00:42:51,903 --> 00:42:54,072
- 15 meters.
- Copy.

795
00:43:02,289 --> 00:43:03,540
[groans]

796
00:43:05,709 --> 00:43:07,877
[bellowing]

797
00:43:15,010 --> 00:43:16,177
[grunts]

798
00:43:16,261 --> 00:43:18,221
- Another dart!
- Okay.

799
00:43:20,307 --> 00:43:21,808
Come on!

800
00:43:24,853 --> 00:43:26,730
[Isabella whimpering]

801
00:43:28,815 --> 00:43:31,067
[bellowing]

802
00:43:31,901 --> 00:43:34,112
It's coming at us! Turn! Turn!

803
00:43:36,698 --> 00:43:38,491
Quick! It's in range.

804
00:43:38,575 --> 00:43:39,784
Come on!

805
00:43:47,709 --> 00:43:48,918
There, go!

806
00:43:54,299 --> 00:43:55,508
[roaring]

807
00:43:55,592 --> 00:43:56,968
[Zora grunting]

808
00:43:57,052 --> 00:43:58,219
[growling]

809
00:43:58,303 --> 00:43:59,303
Hey! [grunts]

810
00:43:59,804 --> 00:44:02,432
Help me up. Help me.

811
00:44:02,515 --> 00:44:04,476
[grunting]

812
00:44:07,312 --> 00:44:08,688
[Krebs] Come on!

813
00:44:13,985 --> 00:44:15,153
Take the shot!

814
00:44:19,115 --> 00:44:20,909
[dart beeping]

815
00:44:21,743 --> 00:44:22,911
[laughs]

816
00:44:22,994 --> 00:44:24,079
[shouts]

817
00:44:26,122 --> 00:44:28,291
[panting]

818
00:44:34,881 --> 00:44:36,299
[shouts]

819
00:44:36,383 --> 00:44:37,384
It worked!

820
00:44:38,176 --> 00:44:40,678
Yes! Come down ten knots.

821
00:44:41,888 --> 00:44:43,598
Hard starboard. Down ten. Down ten.

822
00:44:47,602 --> 00:44:50,897
[Nina] Steady.
Portside off the stern rail.

823
00:44:50,980 --> 00:44:52,899
[panting]

824
00:44:54,734 --> 00:44:56,736
[Loomis] I got it! I got it!

825
00:45:01,950 --> 00:45:02,951
[grunts]

826
00:45:06,871 --> 00:45:08,081
Nice shot.

827
00:45:08,748 --> 00:45:10,041
Nice rocket.

828
00:45:11,084 --> 00:45:12,377
That was fantastic.

829
00:45:12,460 --> 00:45:14,504
- Yeah?
- Oh, my God. That was genius.

830
00:45:14,587 --> 00:45:16,131
[chattering in French]

831
00:45:17,298 --> 00:45:18,466
- Yes.
- That was good.

832
00:45:18,550 --> 00:45:20,301
[LeClerc speaking French]

833
00:45:20,385 --> 00:45:21,386
[Loomis] Oh, God.

834
00:45:21,469 --> 00:45:24,389
[excited chatter continues]

835
00:45:24,472 --> 00:45:25,473
Whoo!

836
00:45:25,557 --> 00:45:26,641
[laughter]

837
00:45:26,724 --> 00:45:31,563
[LeClerc speaking French]

838
00:45:33,440 --> 00:45:35,191
[Reuben] Gotta be calm. Just breathe.

839
00:45:35,275 --> 00:45:36,335
- [Loomis] Hey.
- [Zora] Hey.

840
00:45:36,359 --> 00:45:37,670
- [Reuben] It's okay, honey.
- [Isabella] Dad.

841
00:45:37,694 --> 00:45:39,487
- What if we don't?
- [Reuben] It's okay.

842
00:45:39,571 --> 00:45:40,655
What if we don't what?

843
00:45:41,406 --> 00:45:42,591
Well, what if we get the samples,

844
00:45:42,615 --> 00:45:45,577
and we don't give them over
to a company that makes a lifesaving drug

845
00:45:45,660 --> 00:45:48,830
and then prices it so 99% of the planet
can't afford it?

846
00:45:49,581 --> 00:45:52,250
Science is for all of us, not some of us.

847
00:45:53,126 --> 00:45:54,294
Have you thought about that?

848
00:45:55,503 --> 00:45:56,754
No, I guess I haven't.

849
00:45:58,840 --> 00:46:00,633
Well, then maybe you should start.

850
00:46:01,968 --> 00:46:03,428
Maybe you should stop.

851
00:46:04,304 --> 00:46:06,198
- [Xavier hyperventilating]
- [Reuben] Hey, Xavier.

852
00:46:06,222 --> 00:46:07,807
Head between your legs, deep breaths.

853
00:46:07,891 --> 00:46:09,893
Hey. Deep breaths. Deep breaths.

854
00:46:09,976 --> 00:46:12,437
Dad, I think I'm gonna be sick.

855
00:46:12,520 --> 00:46:14,105
It's all right, honey. Just breathe.

856
00:46:14,189 --> 00:46:15,398
[Isabella breathing heavily]

857
00:46:16,441 --> 00:46:17,901
[latches click, case beeps]

858
00:46:23,615 --> 00:46:26,367
Those things from the boat. They're...

859
00:46:26,451 --> 00:46:27,535
They're back.

860
00:46:35,877 --> 00:46:37,462
- Oh, my God.
- [Isabella] Dad.

861
00:46:37,545 --> 00:46:39,088
What the hell are those?

862
00:46:40,256 --> 00:46:42,550
[Reuben] Sit down, honey. Stay close.

863
00:46:44,302 --> 00:46:45,845
Spinosaurus.

864
00:46:53,686 --> 00:46:55,980
Ha-ha! It's mutualism.

865
00:46:57,649 --> 00:46:59,859
Inter-species symbiosis.

866
00:47:00,902 --> 00:47:04,948
Those Spinosaurus?
They help the Mosasaur hunt.

867
00:47:05,031 --> 00:47:06,115
They help it kill!

868
00:47:08,117 --> 00:47:10,078
- Well, that's discouraging.
- [gun cocks]

869
00:47:10,662 --> 00:47:11,663
[dinosaur grunts]

870
00:47:13,706 --> 00:47:15,416
These are our waters now.

871
00:47:17,001 --> 00:47:18,920
[grunts]

872
00:47:19,003 --> 00:47:20,964
[all gasping]

873
00:47:21,047 --> 00:47:22,687
[Kincaid] Over here. There's another one.

874
00:47:24,801 --> 00:47:27,303
[Krebs] There. Seven o'clock.
Hurry! Quick!

875
00:47:27,387 --> 00:47:29,847
[gunfire]

876
00:47:33,393 --> 00:47:35,311
Bobby. Bobby, Bobby, look out!

877
00:47:35,395 --> 00:47:36,688
[roaring]

878
00:47:36,771 --> 00:47:38,064
[screaming]

879
00:47:39,148 --> 00:47:40,400
No!

880
00:47:41,442 --> 00:47:43,444
[straining]

881
00:47:46,072 --> 00:47:49,117
- [roaring]
- [screaming]

882
00:47:49,200 --> 00:47:51,327
Bobby! Bobby, no!

883
00:47:51,411 --> 00:47:54,497
- [panting]
- [Atwater choking]

884
00:48:00,628 --> 00:48:05,008
[roaring]

885
00:48:05,091 --> 00:48:06,551
[both grunting]

886
00:48:09,387 --> 00:48:10,638
[screams]

887
00:48:12,390 --> 00:48:17,729
[grunting, screaming]

888
00:48:17,812 --> 00:48:19,814
- Dad!
- Bella!

889
00:48:19,897 --> 00:48:21,524
[screaming]

890
00:48:21,608 --> 00:48:22,728
- [straining]
- [bone cracks]

891
00:48:22,775 --> 00:48:24,277
[screams]

892
00:48:24,360 --> 00:48:26,154
[whimpering] Dad!

893
00:48:27,488 --> 00:48:28,906
Dad!

894
00:48:31,326 --> 00:48:33,244
[straining] Oh, my God.

895
00:48:45,923 --> 00:48:47,175
What are you doing?

896
00:48:47,258 --> 00:48:48,458
You're heading into the rocks!

897
00:48:48,509 --> 00:48:50,511
I can outrun those things.

898
00:48:50,595 --> 00:48:53,306
The Mosasaur can't go into shallow water.

899
00:48:54,223 --> 00:48:55,475
[bellowing]

900
00:48:55,558 --> 00:48:57,310
[Kincaid] Everybody hold on to something.

901
00:48:58,936 --> 00:49:01,773
[groaning] I can't move my leg, honey.

902
00:49:01,856 --> 00:49:03,316
[crying]

903
00:49:05,109 --> 00:49:07,278
If we hit those rocks, we're gonna wreck.

904
00:49:08,488 --> 00:49:10,239
Tie these to everything you need.

905
00:49:10,323 --> 00:49:11,741
Be ready to jump.

906
00:49:13,826 --> 00:49:15,995
[bellowing]

907
00:49:18,623 --> 00:49:19,707
It's fast.

908
00:49:21,000 --> 00:49:22,752
LeClerc, the engine's overheating.

909
00:49:28,549 --> 00:49:32,428
Teresa, get back inside. Get on the radio.
Mayday three times and send coordinates.

910
00:49:32,512 --> 00:49:34,312
- Go!
- Three times, send coordinates. Got it.

911
00:49:34,389 --> 00:49:35,515
[Reuben] Hang on, honey.

912
00:49:37,725 --> 00:49:40,395
Isabella. Isabella, hey, look at me.
It's gonna be okay. Listen...

913
00:49:40,478 --> 00:49:42,021
[panting]

914
00:49:42,939 --> 00:49:44,691
- Mayday, Mayday, Mayday.
- Okay. Hang on.

915
00:49:44,774 --> 00:49:46,377
- Hey, hey, hey.
- Is anybody there? Mayday.

916
00:49:46,401 --> 00:49:47,235
- Stop.
- Hey!

917
00:49:47,318 --> 00:49:50,613
- Just stop. Stop for a second.
- What do you mean, "stop"?

918
00:49:50,697 --> 00:49:52,198
We gotta handle this ourselves.

919
00:49:52,281 --> 00:49:54,283
- What?
- This is the protocol.

920
00:49:54,367 --> 00:49:56,285
- Are you crazy?
- Just give me...

921
00:49:56,369 --> 00:49:57,995
No! No, no, no! Hey! Mayday, mayday!

922
00:49:58,579 --> 00:49:59,622
Back off!

923
00:50:01,290 --> 00:50:02,291
[grunts]

924
00:50:02,375 --> 00:50:04,711
[whimpering]

925
00:50:06,212 --> 00:50:07,797
[crying] Please help me!

926
00:50:08,548 --> 00:50:10,800
Please help me! Please!

927
00:50:10,883 --> 00:50:14,095
Please! Please help me!

928
00:50:14,178 --> 00:50:15,763
Grab my hand! Please!

929
00:50:16,556 --> 00:50:18,307
Dad!

930
00:50:18,391 --> 00:50:19,392
[screams]

931
00:50:21,477 --> 00:50:22,478
Sweetie!

932
00:50:22,562 --> 00:50:24,397
Teresa! No!

933
00:50:25,356 --> 00:50:26,399
Teresa!

934
00:50:26,983 --> 00:50:28,109
[grunts]

935
00:50:28,651 --> 00:50:30,069
- [Reuben] Teresa!
- [grunts]

936
00:50:33,948 --> 00:50:35,283
Teresa, no!

937
00:50:35,366 --> 00:50:37,243
[dinosaur bellowing]

938
00:50:37,827 --> 00:50:39,912
Man overboard!

939
00:50:41,247 --> 00:50:43,249
Listen to me.
We're going in the water now.

940
00:50:43,332 --> 00:50:45,728
Yes. The boat's gonna crash.
We have to. Your sister's there.

941
00:50:45,752 --> 00:50:47,545
- You understand? Hey. Hey.
- [whimpering]

942
00:50:47,628 --> 00:50:50,840
Hey. I'm here, okay?
All right? I've got you.

943
00:50:50,923 --> 00:50:53,050
You're all right. Come on! Come on!

944
00:50:54,010 --> 00:50:56,179
Get ready. On the count of three.

945
00:50:56,262 --> 00:50:57,638
One, two...

946
00:50:57,722 --> 00:50:58,723
- Hey!
- Three!

947
00:50:58,806 --> 00:51:00,892
Hey! We need to stick together.

948
00:51:05,563 --> 00:51:06,606
[Loomis] Keep going.

949
00:51:13,988 --> 00:51:16,407
- [bellowing]
- [Nina grunting]

950
00:51:16,991 --> 00:51:18,993
[Zora grunting]

951
00:51:19,076 --> 00:51:20,745
[roaring]

952
00:51:27,668 --> 00:51:29,504
[metal grinding]

953
00:51:30,588 --> 00:51:31,589
Whoa! Get down!

954
00:51:31,672 --> 00:51:34,592
[Zora grunting]

955
00:51:37,345 --> 00:51:38,346
[grunts]

956
00:51:41,307 --> 00:51:43,017
[panting]

957
00:51:44,894 --> 00:51:48,231
- [roaring]
- [engine sputtering]

958
00:51:53,319 --> 00:51:54,570
[both grunt]

959
00:51:54,654 --> 00:51:56,864
Zora. It can't go in the shallows.

960
00:51:56,948 --> 00:51:58,825
Jump. Go! Jump!

961
00:52:06,457 --> 00:52:08,835
[bellowing]

962
00:52:10,211 --> 00:52:12,797
[laughs] Told you!

963
00:52:12,880 --> 00:52:13,881
[shouts]

964
00:52:19,095 --> 00:52:20,847
[shouting]

965
00:52:28,396 --> 00:52:30,022
[seagulls squawking]

966
00:52:47,039 --> 00:52:48,958
[both panting]

967
00:52:51,377 --> 00:52:52,521
[Xavier] I thought I lost you.

968
00:52:52,545 --> 00:52:53,838
Are you okay?

969
00:52:53,921 --> 00:52:55,506
- He let me fall.
- Who?

970
00:52:55,590 --> 00:52:57,192
The guy on the boat. He tried to kill me.

971
00:52:57,216 --> 00:52:58,885
I knew he was creepy!

972
00:52:58,968 --> 00:53:00,428
[Reuben] Teresa. Teresa!

973
00:53:02,722 --> 00:53:03,806
Dad!

974
00:53:11,105 --> 00:53:14,317
- I'm okay. I'm okay.
- Yeah? I got you. I got you. Thank God.

975
00:53:14,400 --> 00:53:15,902
[grunts] You all right? You good?

976
00:53:15,985 --> 00:53:17,361
Let's get out of the water.

977
00:53:29,498 --> 00:53:32,376
[grunting]

978
00:53:33,628 --> 00:53:35,796
[groaning]

979
00:53:35,880 --> 00:53:38,382
[metal creaking]

980
00:53:38,466 --> 00:53:40,927
- [roaring in distance]
- [gasps]

981
00:53:41,010 --> 00:53:43,429
Keep moving! They're amphibious!

982
00:53:45,514 --> 00:53:47,516
[snarling in distance]

983
00:53:52,355 --> 00:53:54,607
[grunting, coughing]

984
00:53:54,690 --> 00:53:56,025
[Kincaid] LeClerc, you okay?

985
00:53:56,776 --> 00:53:58,861
[in French]

986
00:53:58,945 --> 00:54:00,488
[breathing heavily]

987
00:54:00,571 --> 00:54:02,657
[Loomis] Move! Keep going!

988
00:54:05,785 --> 00:54:06,786
[sighs]

989
00:54:14,251 --> 00:54:15,252
[softly] We made it.

990
00:54:15,878 --> 00:54:18,106
- [Loomis] I've got it here.
- Do we still have the case?

991
00:54:18,130 --> 00:54:18,965
[Loomis grunting]

992
00:54:19,048 --> 00:54:20,174
[Zora] You got that side?

993
00:54:20,257 --> 00:54:21,777
- [Loomis] We're getting there. Pull.
- [Zora] Yeah.

994
00:54:21,801 --> 00:54:23,881
- [Nina straining]
- [Loomis] Yeah. Come on. Grab it.

995
00:54:24,762 --> 00:54:27,640
Keep going. Yeah. Go on.
Grab it. Keep moving.

996
00:54:28,891 --> 00:54:30,726
It's all right.
Just keep going to the beach.

997
00:54:30,810 --> 00:54:32,520
[panting]

998
00:54:38,859 --> 00:54:43,531
- [roaring]
- [screaming]

999
00:54:45,074 --> 00:54:46,075
Nina!

1000
00:54:49,370 --> 00:54:51,372
Nina! Nina!

1001
00:54:53,374 --> 00:54:54,583
Nina!

1002
00:54:55,751 --> 00:54:57,044
Nina!

1003
00:54:58,129 --> 00:54:59,130
Nina!

1004
00:54:59,213 --> 00:55:00,214
Kincaid!

1005
00:55:03,592 --> 00:55:04,844
Nina!

1006
00:55:30,286 --> 00:55:31,287
[Isabella crying softly]

1007
00:55:31,370 --> 00:55:35,708
Hey. Hey. I know. I know.
It's okay, honey.

1008
00:55:36,584 --> 00:55:38,044
They said there's a village here.

1009
00:55:38,127 --> 00:55:39,587
We'll find it and get help.

1010
00:55:40,755 --> 00:55:42,506
All right? You don't feel like talking?

1011
00:55:43,632 --> 00:55:45,426
It's okay, honey. You don't have to.

1012
00:55:45,509 --> 00:55:47,053
[panting]

1013
00:55:47,136 --> 00:55:48,220
You'll be okay.

1014
00:55:50,973 --> 00:55:52,183
She'll be okay, right?

1015
00:55:52,266 --> 00:55:54,268
Uh, yeah.

1016
00:55:56,270 --> 00:55:58,439
[birds chirping, calling]

1017
00:56:05,029 --> 00:56:06,030
Oh, wow.

1018
00:56:16,165 --> 00:56:20,294
So, like, when an old person buys a bed,

1019
00:56:22,213 --> 00:56:26,133
do you think before they buy it,
they look at it and they go,

1020
00:56:26,217 --> 00:56:29,512
"Oh, wow, man, this is...
this is my deathbed"?

1021
00:56:29,595 --> 00:56:32,640
[animals calling in distance]

1022
00:56:32,723 --> 00:56:34,308
[Xavier] Because I'm thinking that...

1023
00:56:36,227 --> 00:56:37,228
right now.

1024
00:56:39,563 --> 00:56:40,689
I think we got it.

1025
00:56:42,483 --> 00:56:44,819
Come on. Let's go find this village.

1026
00:56:48,864 --> 00:56:50,104
- Hurry up.
- [roar in distance]

1027
00:56:50,157 --> 00:56:51,700
We can't stay here. They'll come back.

1028
00:56:51,784 --> 00:56:53,887
- [Krebs] They're more like mutations.
- What do you mean, "mutations"?

1029
00:56:53,911 --> 00:56:55,079
Abnormalities, deviations.

1030
00:56:55,162 --> 00:56:56,372
I know what the word means.

1031
00:56:56,455 --> 00:56:58,290
What does it mean here, where we are?

1032
00:56:58,374 --> 00:57:00,811
- [Kincaid] What are you talking about?
- This island that we're on,

1033
00:57:00,835 --> 00:57:03,420
Mr. Roarke here didn't tell us everything
that we need to know.

1034
00:57:03,504 --> 00:57:05,690
Look, dinosaurs are dinosaurs.
What difference does it make?

1035
00:57:05,714 --> 00:57:07,859
Fine. Don't tell us.
We could just leave you here. It's fine.

1036
00:57:07,883 --> 00:57:11,428
Fine. So this island was
a laboratory of sorts.

1037
00:57:11,929 --> 00:57:14,098
They conducted experimental work here.

1038
00:57:15,266 --> 00:57:16,392
What kind of experiments?

1039
00:57:16,475 --> 00:57:17,852
Crossbreeding of species.

1040
00:57:17,935 --> 00:57:21,063
The theme park owners were just responding
to what the audience wanted.

1041
00:57:21,147 --> 00:57:23,315
They were tired of looking
at the same old thing.

1042
00:57:23,399 --> 00:57:25,693
"Engineered entertainments,"
they called 'em.

1043
00:57:25,776 --> 00:57:27,278
Genetically-altered freaks?

1044
00:57:27,945 --> 00:57:30,948
Yeah. And you don't want to do that
in the middle of a theme park, do you?

1045
00:57:31,031 --> 00:57:33,075
Ideally, you don't try
weird genetic shit at all.

1046
00:57:33,159 --> 00:57:35,077
Well, they learned that the hard way.

1047
00:57:35,161 --> 00:57:39,456
Any that were malformed or just
too damn hard for anybody to look at,

1048
00:57:40,499 --> 00:57:41,500
they left 'em here.

1049
00:57:41,584 --> 00:57:43,919
[animals calling in distance]

1050
00:57:44,003 --> 00:57:45,123
[Loomis] But that's inhuman.

1051
00:57:45,754 --> 00:57:47,006
Why not just euthanize them?

1052
00:57:47,089 --> 00:57:50,968
The average cost of
a created species is 72 million dollars.

1053
00:57:51,051 --> 00:57:53,220
What would you do,
kill it and have to tell your bank,

1054
00:57:53,304 --> 00:57:54,864
or just carry it forward under R and D?

1055
00:57:54,930 --> 00:57:57,933
What would I do with mutant dinosaurs
from an accounting perspective?

1056
00:57:58,017 --> 00:57:59,310
Is that really the question?

1057
00:57:59,393 --> 00:58:01,979
[roaring in distance]

1058
00:58:02,062 --> 00:58:03,564
Can we please just hurry this along?

1059
00:58:03,647 --> 00:58:05,274
Everybody, please stay cool.

1060
00:58:05,357 --> 00:58:06,859
Our situation hasn't changed.

1061
00:58:06,942 --> 00:58:10,321
Two of our friends just died. Clearly,
the situation's changed quite a bit.

1062
00:58:10,404 --> 00:58:11,739
Yes, and that's horrible.

1063
00:58:11,822 --> 00:58:15,409
But this is where we are now, desperate,
and that's not any different than before.

1064
00:58:15,492 --> 00:58:18,579
We were all desperate to begin with,
otherwise we wouldn't have come. Right?

1065
00:58:18,662 --> 00:58:21,040
You, you specialize
in a subject nobody cares about anymore.

1066
00:58:21,123 --> 00:58:23,250
You're about to be out of a job.
You need this.

1067
00:58:23,334 --> 00:58:25,127
I want the money. You want the money.

1068
00:58:25,211 --> 00:58:26,754
Your company wants all the money.

1069
00:58:26,837 --> 00:58:28,088
We still have that case.

1070
00:58:28,172 --> 00:58:29,972
First sample's in it.
Let's get the other two.

1071
00:58:30,049 --> 00:58:31,300
[Krebs] That's a great idea.

1072
00:58:31,967 --> 00:58:33,636
[Kincaid] We have no weapons, Z.

1073
00:58:34,428 --> 00:58:35,930
We never did. I mean, not really.

1074
00:58:36,013 --> 00:58:38,682
We had a few toys
that would have made us feel better.

1075
00:58:38,766 --> 00:58:41,477
They could have worked or not.
We are where we're supposed to be.

1076
00:58:41,560 --> 00:58:44,647
We know what we're supposed to do.
Let's just get what we need and get out.

1077
00:58:44,730 --> 00:58:47,858
Those kids, the people that went
into the ocean, they could still be alive.

1078
00:58:47,942 --> 00:58:49,985
I say we find them first
and then we get out.

1079
00:58:50,069 --> 00:58:51,487
Uh, get out how?

1080
00:58:52,071 --> 00:58:53,631
Does anyone wanna hear the backup plan?

1081
00:58:53,656 --> 00:58:55,133
[Krebs chuckles] She has a backup plan?

1082
00:58:55,157 --> 00:58:57,576
She doesn't get out of bed
without a backup plan.

1083
00:59:01,288 --> 00:59:05,292
I hired an exfil team
to monitor our boat's radio frequency.

1084
00:59:05,376 --> 00:59:07,753
So if we go silent for 24 hours,

1085
00:59:07,836 --> 00:59:10,631
they'll do a flyby at exactly sunset
on the second night.

1086
00:59:11,131 --> 00:59:12,466
But that's tomorrow.

1087
00:59:13,467 --> 00:59:17,054
Your plans showed a village complex
on the southwest ridge with a helipad.

1088
00:59:17,137 --> 00:59:19,699
They'll hover for exactly two minutes.
If no one shows, they'll leave.

1089
00:59:19,723 --> 00:59:21,892
- But if they see us...
- Why only two minutes?

1090
00:59:21,976 --> 00:59:23,269
Why don't they just land?

1091
00:59:23,352 --> 00:59:24,353
Pilots wanna live too.

1092
00:59:24,436 --> 00:59:25,729
[Loomis] Oh, great.

1093
00:59:26,230 --> 00:59:28,857
We gotta find high ground
before the sun goes down.

1094
00:59:28,941 --> 00:59:31,277
[animals calling]

1095
00:59:32,820 --> 00:59:35,906
[dinosaur roaring in distance]

1096
00:59:35,990 --> 00:59:37,074
We have to go now.

1097
00:59:37,157 --> 00:59:39,243
[Zora] Drop anything
you can't carry all day.

1098
00:59:41,578 --> 00:59:42,746
[in French]

1099
00:59:43,330 --> 00:59:44,999
Just try not to die.

1100
00:59:45,082 --> 00:59:46,542
[panting]

1101
00:59:46,625 --> 00:59:49,253
[adventurous music playing]

1102
01:00:05,811 --> 01:00:06,812
Warm.

1103
01:00:07,313 --> 01:00:09,440
There's geothermal power.

1104
01:00:10,399 --> 01:00:12,359
Must come through these pipes
from hot springs.

1105
01:00:14,486 --> 01:00:15,988
It must lead to the village.

1106
01:00:16,989 --> 01:00:18,073
[Teresa grunting]

1107
01:00:18,157 --> 01:00:19,491
Maybe there's a radio?

1108
01:00:20,367 --> 01:00:22,369
Great. [chuckles] Yeah.

1109
01:00:22,453 --> 01:00:24,580
We'll use that to get the guy
who tried to kill me.

1110
01:00:25,581 --> 01:00:26,874
Or, you know, to get rescued?

1111
01:00:26,957 --> 01:00:29,752
Yeah. Then we'll get the son of a bitch.

1112
01:00:31,003 --> 01:00:33,964
Right. Okay, we follow the pipes
to the village.

1113
01:00:34,048 --> 01:00:36,008
Unless somebody's got another idea.

1114
01:00:37,426 --> 01:00:38,719
I have some weed.

1115
01:00:41,263 --> 01:00:43,182
Might be a little damp, but, uh,

1116
01:00:44,058 --> 01:00:46,018
I feel like we could just chill here, no?

1117
01:00:46,101 --> 01:00:47,853
You don't smoke, you... Do you smoke?

1118
01:00:49,980 --> 01:00:50,981
No?

1119
01:00:53,567 --> 01:00:55,903
[laughs]

1120
01:00:55,986 --> 01:00:58,447
Okay, Dad, he's, uh...

1121
01:00:59,156 --> 01:01:00,908
he's clearly messing with you.

1122
01:01:02,701 --> 01:01:03,702
Oh.

1123
01:01:04,536 --> 01:01:05,537
[chuckles]

1124
01:01:06,663 --> 01:01:08,290
- [sighs]
- [Xavier] Yo, help me up?

1125
01:01:08,374 --> 01:01:09,375
Funny.

1126
01:01:11,085 --> 01:01:12,961
All right. No one help me up. That's fine.

1127
01:01:28,519 --> 01:01:30,813
[insects chirring]

1128
01:01:31,772 --> 01:01:34,483
[Zora] Maybe that family will know
to head for the village.

1129
01:01:34,566 --> 01:01:35,818
[Kincaid] God, I hope so.

1130
01:01:35,901 --> 01:01:38,028
[animals calling in distance]

1131
01:01:40,239 --> 01:01:41,990
[Zora] How far to the next sample?

1132
01:01:42,074 --> 01:01:43,593
Well, according to the satellite imagery,

1133
01:01:43,617 --> 01:01:45,994
the Titanosaur herd stays
in the central valley.

1134
01:01:47,162 --> 01:01:48,747
We should reach them by dawn.

1135
01:01:51,375 --> 01:01:53,460
- [animal chitters]
- [gasps]

1136
01:01:53,544 --> 01:01:56,171
Be careful where you step.
Something just moved past my leg.

1137
01:01:58,674 --> 01:02:01,510
Hey. You wanna tell me how
the girl fell over the rail?

1138
01:02:02,219 --> 01:02:04,179
The thing hit the boat. You were there.

1139
01:02:04,263 --> 01:02:06,932
But so were you,
on the bridge, next to her.

1140
01:02:07,015 --> 01:02:09,059
So there was nothing you could do?

1141
01:02:09,852 --> 01:02:11,103
What are you implying?

1142
01:02:14,356 --> 01:02:16,108
This isn't your first expedition, huh?

1143
01:02:16,191 --> 01:02:17,860
No, I've been on digs since I was 12.

1144
01:02:19,027 --> 01:02:21,155
I studied under Alan Grant as a postdoc.

1145
01:02:22,489 --> 01:02:24,908
I suppose you're
in this kind of situation all the time?

1146
01:02:24,992 --> 01:02:26,994
[chuckles] Used to be.

1147
01:02:27,077 --> 01:02:29,246
I hate the jungle. I try to avoid it now.

1148
01:02:29,872 --> 01:02:30,873
Why is that?

1149
01:02:30,956 --> 01:02:34,626
You can't see three feet in front of you,
and you always know you're being stalked.

1150
01:02:35,461 --> 01:02:37,588
And the only place to hide is underwater.

1151
01:02:39,256 --> 01:02:40,674
I refuse to die in the jungle.

1152
01:02:42,676 --> 01:02:45,762
My dream is I die in a shallow sea,
and I'm buried quickly by silt.

1153
01:02:47,973 --> 01:02:48,974
That's beautiful.

1154
01:02:49,057 --> 01:02:51,810
It's the best chance
of being fossilized that way.

1155
01:02:51,894 --> 01:02:53,687
[Kincaid laughing]

1156
01:02:53,770 --> 01:02:54,771
You're a weirdo.

1157
01:02:56,273 --> 01:02:57,274
Thank you.

1158
01:03:00,819 --> 01:03:01,880
[Krebs] You gotta drop this.

1159
01:03:01,904 --> 01:03:05,115
She screamed,
I turned around, and then she was gone.

1160
01:03:05,199 --> 01:03:06,241
[Zora] Okay. All right.

1161
01:03:06,325 --> 01:03:08,386
If I find out otherwise, though,
we'll leave you here

1162
01:03:08,410 --> 01:03:10,204
and you can rejoin the food chain.

1163
01:03:10,287 --> 01:03:12,498
[chuckles] I'm too smart to die.

1164
01:03:12,581 --> 01:03:15,292
You know, intelligence is massively
overrated as an adaptive trait.

1165
01:03:15,375 --> 01:03:17,044
- Oh, yeah?
- Seriously.

1166
01:03:17,127 --> 01:03:18,128
Enlighten us.

1167
01:03:18,212 --> 01:03:20,839
Dinosaurs, pretty dumb, right?

1168
01:03:20,923 --> 01:03:23,759
And yet they survived
for 167 million years.

1169
01:03:23,842 --> 01:03:27,679
And we Homo sapiens,
geniuses by comparison,

1170
01:03:27,763 --> 01:03:30,641
only have about 200,000 years so far.

1171
01:03:30,724 --> 01:03:34,770
But with our huge cranial cavities,
we're so smart,

1172
01:03:34,853 --> 01:03:37,773
we already have
the capacity to annihilate ourselves.

1173
01:03:39,650 --> 01:03:41,485
I doubt we make it to even one million.

1174
01:03:42,528 --> 01:03:45,697
Hold on. Don't we rule the earth?
We gotta be doing something right.

1175
01:03:46,323 --> 01:03:47,491
We don't rule the earth.

1176
01:03:48,283 --> 01:03:49,368
We just think we do.

1177
01:03:50,994 --> 01:03:53,038
I mean, sure,
we're changing the environment,

1178
01:03:53,121 --> 01:03:55,916
but that makes us the ones
to worry about, not the planet.

1179
01:03:56,500 --> 01:03:58,502
When the Earth gets tired of us,
believe me,

1180
01:03:58,585 --> 01:04:02,005
it will shake us off like a summer cold.

1181
01:04:05,175 --> 01:04:07,511
Of all species that have existed on Earth,

1182
01:04:07,594 --> 01:04:10,430
99.9% of them are now extinct.

1183
01:04:13,684 --> 01:04:15,102
Survival is a long shot.

1184
01:04:16,228 --> 01:04:17,229
[sighs]

1185
01:04:19,231 --> 01:04:20,315
[water splashing]

1186
01:04:20,399 --> 01:04:21,399
Whoa.

1187
01:04:25,195 --> 01:04:26,321
You hear that?

1188
01:04:27,030 --> 01:04:28,448
Every day could be your last.

1189
01:04:36,748 --> 01:04:38,625
All right, everyone, find a stick.

1190
01:04:39,418 --> 01:04:41,128
You'll need it to clear your way.

1191
01:04:49,761 --> 01:04:51,722
[grunts, gasps]

1192
01:04:51,805 --> 01:04:54,725
- [panting]
- [chittering]

1193
01:04:56,351 --> 01:04:57,352
[Reuben] Bella?

1194
01:04:58,645 --> 01:04:59,730
It's okay.

1195
01:05:00,314 --> 01:05:01,315
It's little.

1196
01:05:02,608 --> 01:05:04,401
See? And it's a plant-eater.

1197
01:05:04,484 --> 01:05:05,485
It's okay.

1198
01:05:05,569 --> 01:05:07,446
No. No. Go.

1199
01:05:07,529 --> 01:05:09,990
- Go. Shoo.
- [dinosaur whimpering]

1200
01:05:13,035 --> 01:05:15,203
- [grunts] Hey, Xavier.
- Yeah.

1201
01:05:16,163 --> 01:05:17,748
You jumped in after her.

1202
01:05:17,831 --> 01:05:18,832
Yeah.

1203
01:05:19,333 --> 01:05:20,917
Respect for that.

1204
01:05:21,001 --> 01:05:22,502
[chuckles]

1205
01:05:22,586 --> 01:05:23,962
Whatever, <i>guapo.</i>

1206
01:05:30,344 --> 01:05:31,595
Hard to like, honey.

1207
01:05:31,678 --> 01:05:32,679
Yeah.

1208
01:05:32,763 --> 01:05:33,889
Took me a minute, too.

1209
01:05:35,974 --> 01:05:37,225
Let's do this.

1210
01:05:38,477 --> 01:05:40,103
[grunting]

1211
01:05:40,187 --> 01:05:43,023
[dinosaur grumbling]

1212
01:05:50,197 --> 01:05:52,757
Let's do this... [stammers]
...but, like, going that way, though.

1213
01:06:00,916 --> 01:06:02,709
[Zora] What are the last two species?

1214
01:06:02,793 --> 01:06:05,212
Uh, Titanosaur and Quetzalcoatlus.

1215
01:06:05,295 --> 01:06:06,505
An 11-ton sauropod

1216
01:06:06,588 --> 01:06:09,675
and a pterosaur the size of a school bus
with 30-foot wings.

1217
01:06:09,758 --> 01:06:12,886
[Kincaid] One of those little buses
like for field trips, or the full thing?

1218
01:06:12,969 --> 01:06:14,137
[Krebs] Does it matter?

1219
01:06:14,805 --> 01:06:15,806
[Kincaid] Just asking.

1220
01:06:42,207 --> 01:06:45,252
[deep metallic creaking]

1221
01:07:09,776 --> 01:07:12,904
[animals chittering, calling]

1222
01:07:16,074 --> 01:07:18,577
[dinosaur grumbling softly]

1223
01:07:20,704 --> 01:07:22,539
[panting]

1224
01:07:24,374 --> 01:07:26,460
[fire crackling softly]

1225
01:07:33,383 --> 01:07:34,384
It's okay.

1226
01:07:36,636 --> 01:07:39,181
You get some more sleep
while I take it for a while.

1227
01:07:39,264 --> 01:07:40,974
Oh, no. I'm okay.

1228
01:07:41,057 --> 01:07:42,893
I know you're okay, but it's my watch.

1229
01:07:46,021 --> 01:07:47,272
You sure?

1230
01:07:47,355 --> 01:07:48,356
Yeah.

1231
01:07:48,440 --> 01:07:50,650
[groans] Thanks.

1232
01:07:51,943 --> 01:07:54,029
The pipes, they just go right up that way,

1233
01:07:55,113 --> 01:07:56,448
hopefully towards the village.

1234
01:07:56,531 --> 01:07:57,532
All right?

1235
01:08:04,372 --> 01:08:06,124
You know, I was wrong about you.

1236
01:08:08,001 --> 01:08:10,462
No, you're right. I'm lazy as hell.
[chuckles]

1237
01:08:13,048 --> 01:08:15,008
She might be alive
because of what you did.

1238
01:08:21,598 --> 01:08:25,310
I don't even know why
she wants to be with me. [chuckles]

1239
01:08:26,937 --> 01:08:29,105
Well, she sees who you are.

1240
01:08:31,566 --> 01:08:32,692
Thank God she doesn't.

1241
01:08:35,529 --> 01:08:36,530
Hey.

1242
01:08:37,113 --> 01:08:38,113
Hey!

1243
01:08:38,782 --> 01:08:40,700
Other people may talk shit about us,

1244
01:08:41,368 --> 01:08:43,119
but we don't have to do the job for them.

1245
01:08:44,120 --> 01:08:45,831
Otherwise it becomes true.

1246
01:08:54,089 --> 01:08:55,465
Well, I gotta take a piss.

1247
01:09:04,975 --> 01:09:07,352
[breathes deeply]

1248
01:09:14,776 --> 01:09:16,987
[dinosaur chittering]

1249
01:09:19,739 --> 01:09:22,075
[leaves rustling]

1250
01:09:28,206 --> 01:09:30,125
[chittering]

1251
01:09:55,108 --> 01:09:56,318
[clicking tongue]

1252
01:09:56,401 --> 01:09:59,112
[clicking tongue] Come on.

1253
01:10:00,155 --> 01:10:01,156
Come.

1254
01:10:02,198 --> 01:10:03,199
It's okay.

1255
01:10:05,076 --> 01:10:06,828
Come on. [clicking tongue]

1256
01:10:16,421 --> 01:10:17,672
[chuckles softly]

1257
01:10:26,890 --> 01:10:28,183
[dinosaur calling in distance]

1258
01:10:42,948 --> 01:10:45,909
- [sighs]
- [urinating]

1259
01:10:48,161 --> 01:10:49,663
[growling softly]

1260
01:10:51,790 --> 01:10:53,041
[exhales]

1261
01:10:53,124 --> 01:10:54,209
[spits]

1262
01:10:57,128 --> 01:10:58,129
[sniffs]

1263
01:10:58,838 --> 01:10:59,839
[hissing]

1264
01:10:59,923 --> 01:11:02,801
- [dinosaurs shrieking]
- [urinating stops]

1265
01:11:06,805 --> 01:11:07,847
[whimpers]

1266
01:11:07,931 --> 01:11:10,016
[shrieking continues]

1267
01:11:10,100 --> 01:11:12,102
[slashing, crunching]

1268
01:11:15,897 --> 01:11:17,273
- [roars]
- [grunts]

1269
01:11:17,357 --> 01:11:19,109
[slashing]

1270
01:11:24,447 --> 01:11:26,825
- [breathing heavily]
- [squawks]

1271
01:11:32,580 --> 01:11:36,209
[panting]

1272
01:11:39,087 --> 01:11:42,298
- [urinating]
- [panting]

1273
01:11:52,017 --> 01:11:53,184
[Krebs] How much further?

1274
01:11:53,268 --> 01:11:54,686
[Loomis] Not far.

1275
01:11:54,769 --> 01:11:57,564
The Titanosaur herd
should be right across this valley.

1276
01:11:57,647 --> 01:11:59,315
[Zora] But they're herbivores, right?

1277
01:11:59,399 --> 01:12:00,525
[Loomis] Yeah.

1278
01:12:00,608 --> 01:12:01,818
[Zora] That's good.

1279
01:12:01,901 --> 01:12:04,195
[Loomis] But the things
that hunt them aren't.

1280
01:12:04,279 --> 01:12:05,280
[Krebs] Oh, great.

1281
01:12:05,363 --> 01:12:06,531
[Kincaid shushing]

1282
01:12:06,614 --> 01:12:07,615
[whispers] Quiet.

1283
01:12:08,658 --> 01:12:12,078
[dinosaurs breathing slowly]

1284
01:12:24,591 --> 01:12:26,926
[dinosaurs continue breathing slowly]

1285
01:12:48,490 --> 01:12:50,742
[dinosaur bellowing faintly in distance]

1286
01:12:56,915 --> 01:12:59,459
[dinosaurs bellowing faintly in distance]

1287
01:13:04,214 --> 01:13:05,840
[bellowing, slow breathing stop]

1288
01:13:07,342 --> 01:13:09,219
[mint crunching]

1289
01:13:09,302 --> 01:13:11,429
[Zora sighs] Mmm.

1290
01:13:12,889 --> 01:13:14,724
You want one? Mmm.

1291
01:13:14,808 --> 01:13:16,017
[sighs]

1292
01:13:17,727 --> 01:13:19,437
[grass rustling]

1293
01:13:26,402 --> 01:13:27,403
[gasps]

1294
01:13:27,487 --> 01:13:29,864
[bellowing softly]

1295
01:13:29,948 --> 01:13:32,033
Whoa, whoa, whoa, whoa, whoa, whoa.

1296
01:13:32,117 --> 01:13:34,077
Oh. [chuckles]

1297
01:13:34,160 --> 01:13:37,413
- [bellowing]
- [birds chirping]

1298
01:13:38,915 --> 01:13:40,667
[panting] What?

1299
01:13:43,044 --> 01:13:44,587
[bellowing]

1300
01:13:44,671 --> 01:13:45,839
Whoa, whoa, whoa.

1301
01:14:03,898 --> 01:14:05,817
[birds chirping]

1302
01:14:12,240 --> 01:14:14,284
You're a secret adrenaline junkie, Henry?

1303
01:14:17,537 --> 01:14:19,664
This is our chance. They're distracted.

1304
01:14:19,747 --> 01:14:20,957
Distracted by what?

1305
01:14:21,833 --> 01:14:23,835
[dinosaur bellowing]

1306
01:14:25,170 --> 01:14:27,338
[gasps] Oh.

1307
01:14:27,422 --> 01:14:29,841
[panting]

1308
01:14:30,633 --> 01:14:31,634
Okay.

1309
01:14:33,386 --> 01:14:35,889
[dinosaurs bellowing]

1310
01:14:35,972 --> 01:14:38,600
[hopeful music playing]

1311
01:14:53,948 --> 01:14:55,283
You don't see that every day.

1312
01:14:56,367 --> 01:14:57,827
Or ever.

1313
01:14:57,911 --> 01:15:00,163
[breathing shakily]

1314
01:15:24,896 --> 01:15:27,065
[sniffling]

1315
01:15:34,864 --> 01:15:37,075
[panting]

1316
01:15:38,618 --> 01:15:40,995
[bellowing continues]

1317
01:15:56,469 --> 01:15:58,263
[purring]

1318
01:16:12,652 --> 01:16:14,696
[bellows sharply]

1319
01:16:23,913 --> 01:16:25,873
[laughs]

1320
01:16:26,582 --> 01:16:29,585
[bellowing]

1321
01:16:30,545 --> 01:16:33,423
[chuckles] Whoo!

1322
01:16:33,506 --> 01:16:35,341
- [dart beeping]
- [dinosaur grunts]

1323
01:16:35,425 --> 01:16:38,052
[sweeping music playing]

1324
01:16:41,556 --> 01:16:42,557
[sighs]

1325
01:16:49,731 --> 01:16:52,150
[sweeping music continues]

1326
01:17:28,102 --> 01:17:29,103
[case beeps]

1327
01:17:29,187 --> 01:17:30,938
[dinosaurs squawking in distance]

1328
01:17:34,484 --> 01:17:35,651
One to go.

1329
01:17:38,237 --> 01:17:39,530
Looks like we're climbing.

1330
01:17:40,239 --> 01:17:42,033
[Kincaid] Hope you're good
with heights, Doc.

1331
01:17:51,542 --> 01:17:54,142
[Reuben] The guys on the boat
said there was power at the village.

1332
01:17:54,170 --> 01:17:56,047
We just have to trust
that they were right.

1333
01:17:56,130 --> 01:17:57,775
[Xavier] Hey, how much further
do you think it is?

1334
01:17:57,799 --> 01:17:58,925
A mile, maybe two.

1335
01:18:00,635 --> 01:18:02,470
[groans, grunts]

1336
01:18:02,553 --> 01:18:03,554
Is your leg okay?

1337
01:18:03,638 --> 01:18:06,015
Yeah, it just hurts a little. I'm fine.

1338
01:18:06,099 --> 01:18:07,100
It's okay.

1339
01:18:07,183 --> 01:18:09,435
[dinosaur chittering]

1340
01:18:09,519 --> 01:18:10,770
[Teresa] And, uh...

1341
01:18:11,604 --> 01:18:15,691
are we okay with... that?

1342
01:18:15,775 --> 01:18:18,152
[smacking lips]

1343
01:18:20,029 --> 01:18:21,322
It's helping her.

1344
01:18:23,157 --> 01:18:24,826
It's okay. I got an eye on it.

1345
01:18:26,035 --> 01:18:28,663
[grunting]

1346
01:18:32,208 --> 01:18:34,043
Mom's never gonna trust you with us again.

1347
01:18:34,127 --> 01:18:35,253
[chuckles] Oh, God, no.

1348
01:18:36,045 --> 01:18:37,338
[Isabella] Hey, guys.

1349
01:18:38,464 --> 01:18:40,425
I'm naming her Dolores.

1350
01:18:40,508 --> 01:18:41,843
[Xavier] Iz, you're alive?

1351
01:18:42,510 --> 01:18:43,886
You talk?

1352
01:18:44,762 --> 01:18:46,722
I'm taking her home with me.

1353
01:18:46,806 --> 01:18:48,891
That may or may not be a terrible idea.

1354
01:18:48,975 --> 01:18:50,059
Dolores?

1355
01:18:52,061 --> 01:18:53,062
Hey, you good?

1356
01:18:53,146 --> 01:18:56,566
Yeah, it's, uh...
It's just kinda nice, you and your dad.

1357
01:18:56,649 --> 01:18:59,026
I don't know.
Maybe this whole thing will... [grunting]

1358
01:18:59,110 --> 01:19:00,153
Xavier!

1359
01:19:00,236 --> 01:19:01,904
[Reuben] Bella, Bella! Bella, wait!

1360
01:19:01,988 --> 01:19:03,573
- [Isabella screams]
- [Reuben grunts]

1361
01:19:03,656 --> 01:19:06,033
[all grunting]

1362
01:19:06,117 --> 01:19:08,453
[dramatic music playing]

1363
01:19:08,536 --> 01:19:10,455
- [Xavier grunts]
- [Teresa yelps]

1364
01:19:13,249 --> 01:19:14,542
[Reuben groans]

1365
01:19:15,209 --> 01:19:16,961
[Isabella yelps]

1366
01:19:17,044 --> 01:19:19,338
[all breathing heavily]

1367
01:19:21,883 --> 01:19:23,718
[Dolores chittering]

1368
01:19:30,224 --> 01:19:31,476
What's the smell?

1369
01:19:33,186 --> 01:19:34,896
[chittering]

1370
01:19:36,355 --> 01:19:38,024
[Isabella] What's wrong with her?

1371
01:19:44,947 --> 01:19:46,365
What is she scared of?

1372
01:19:46,449 --> 01:19:49,202
[tense music playing]

1373
01:19:50,077 --> 01:19:51,287
[Reuben] Help me up.

1374
01:19:52,872 --> 01:19:54,582
[grunts]

1375
01:19:54,665 --> 01:19:56,792
[panting]

1376
01:19:58,377 --> 01:20:00,880
We'll follow the river that way.

1377
01:20:01,756 --> 01:20:02,882
I see a boat.

1378
01:20:04,342 --> 01:20:05,510
You can't walk.

1379
01:20:05,593 --> 01:20:07,929
Xavier can help you while I go get it.

1380
01:20:08,679 --> 01:20:10,598
All right. Be careful.

1381
01:20:16,854 --> 01:20:18,272
Can I get some more licorice?

1382
01:20:20,483 --> 01:20:21,609
[sighs]

1383
01:20:31,661 --> 01:20:32,662
[whimpers]

1384
01:20:33,704 --> 01:20:36,123
[tense music playing]

1385
01:20:38,376 --> 01:20:40,169
- [insect buzzes]
- [whispers] Oh, my God.

1386
01:20:44,465 --> 01:20:45,550
[grunts]

1387
01:21:03,401 --> 01:21:05,236
[Reuben whispering] Teresa.

1388
01:21:05,319 --> 01:21:07,613
Teresa! Hurry.

1389
01:21:07,697 --> 01:21:09,699
Just go. Come on.

1390
01:21:15,204 --> 01:21:17,331
[flies buzzing]

1391
01:21:23,212 --> 01:21:24,839
[chewing]

1392
01:21:24,922 --> 01:21:27,008
[dinosaur grunting]

1393
01:21:27,091 --> 01:21:29,385
[chirping]

1394
01:21:32,305 --> 01:21:35,099
[hissing, rattling]

1395
01:21:35,182 --> 01:21:36,309
Careful.

1396
01:21:37,560 --> 01:21:40,521
- [dinosaur shrieks]
- [dinosaur grumbles]

1397
01:21:40,605 --> 01:21:42,023
[whimpers]

1398
01:21:42,898 --> 01:21:44,567
[squeals]

1399
01:21:44,650 --> 01:21:46,736
[panting softly]

1400
01:21:46,819 --> 01:21:49,530
[dinosaur breathing slowly]

1401
01:21:50,406 --> 01:21:52,617
[grass rustling]

1402
01:21:52,700 --> 01:21:55,202
[low growling]

1403
01:21:59,040 --> 01:22:01,042
[yawning]

1404
01:22:07,423 --> 01:22:09,258
[yawning]

1405
01:22:12,219 --> 01:22:13,280
[whispering] Come on. Come on.

1406
01:22:13,304 --> 01:22:15,024
- Shh. Don't wake it up.
- Come on. Come on.

1407
01:22:15,890 --> 01:22:18,976
[growling softly]

1408
01:22:19,644 --> 01:22:20,895
[whispering] Move! Come back.

1409
01:22:20,978 --> 01:22:22,438
[panting softly]

1410
01:22:25,941 --> 01:22:26,942
Okay.

1411
01:22:33,532 --> 01:22:34,533
No.

1412
01:22:35,493 --> 01:22:36,494
Shit.

1413
01:22:36,577 --> 01:22:38,704
- [high-pitched chittering]
- [gasps]

1414
01:22:48,464 --> 01:22:49,799
[Dolores chittering]

1415
01:22:49,882 --> 01:22:51,050
[whispering] Dolores!

1416
01:22:53,678 --> 01:22:55,304
You have to go. It's not safe.

1417
01:22:58,766 --> 01:23:00,685
[snoring]

1418
01:23:05,106 --> 01:23:06,232
[whimpers softly]

1419
01:23:08,442 --> 01:23:10,569
Please, go, go, go, go, go.

1420
01:23:10,653 --> 01:23:12,697
Shoo! Shoo!

1421
01:23:12,780 --> 01:23:15,116
[grunting]

1422
01:23:19,328 --> 01:23:20,329
Come on. Come on.

1423
01:23:25,710 --> 01:23:28,629
[grunting]

1424
01:23:31,757 --> 01:23:32,925
[whispering] No. Don't.

1425
01:23:33,008 --> 01:23:34,343
No, don't. Don't. Don't.

1426
01:23:38,013 --> 01:23:40,558
[air whooshing]

1427
01:23:41,559 --> 01:23:42,560
[grunts]

1428
01:23:46,856 --> 01:23:48,107
[Reuben] Teresa, come on.

1429
01:23:49,066 --> 01:23:50,359
Come on.

1430
01:23:51,193 --> 01:23:52,737
Come on, baby. Come on!

1431
01:23:52,820 --> 01:23:54,029
Come on!

1432
01:23:54,113 --> 01:23:55,489
[grunts]

1433
01:23:56,866 --> 01:23:58,451
[dinosaur grunts]

1434
01:23:59,368 --> 01:24:01,454
[growling breaths]

1435
01:24:07,001 --> 01:24:08,377
[whispering] Come on! Come on!

1436
01:24:14,300 --> 01:24:15,634
Okay.

1437
01:24:15,718 --> 01:24:17,052
[dinosaur squawking]

1438
01:24:18,554 --> 01:24:19,930
[dinosaur grunts]

1439
01:24:20,514 --> 01:24:21,515
Isabella.

1440
01:24:21,599 --> 01:24:23,017
Come on. Come on.

1441
01:24:23,100 --> 01:24:25,728
[dramatic music playing]

1442
01:24:28,522 --> 01:24:29,815
[Reuben grunting]

1443
01:24:29,899 --> 01:24:32,359
All right, sit in the front now.

1444
01:24:32,443 --> 01:24:34,653
- Let's go. Go.
- [bellowing]

1445
01:24:34,737 --> 01:24:35,738
[Reuben] Quietly.

1446
01:24:38,365 --> 01:24:40,284
- [shouting] Go! Go! Go!
- [Isabella whimpering]

1447
01:24:41,076 --> 01:24:42,286
[Teresa] Paddle!

1448
01:24:42,369 --> 01:24:43,746
Paddle. Come on.

1449
01:24:45,039 --> 01:24:47,041
[Xavier grunting]

1450
01:24:47,124 --> 01:24:48,727
- [Isabella] Dad!
- [Reuben] Okay, okay, okay. Okay.

1451
01:24:48,751 --> 01:24:49,794
Come on, come on, come on!

1452
01:24:49,877 --> 01:24:53,047
[shouting] Paddle, paddle, paddle!

1453
01:24:54,340 --> 01:24:55,633
Dad!

1454
01:24:56,675 --> 01:24:58,487
- [Teresa] Go!
- [Xavier] Come on. Come on. Come on.

1455
01:24:58,511 --> 01:25:00,113
- [Isabella] Go!
- [Reuben] Come on. Come on.

1456
01:25:00,137 --> 01:25:01,347
Paddle!

1457
01:25:01,972 --> 01:25:03,849
- [grunting]
- [Xavier] Dig, dig!

1458
01:25:03,933 --> 01:25:06,101
Dig! Dig! Come on!

1459
01:25:08,854 --> 01:25:09,939
Come on, come on, come on.

1460
01:25:14,443 --> 01:25:16,654
[Reuben] As hard as you can! Dig! Dig!

1461
01:25:26,288 --> 01:25:27,873
Come on! Paddle!

1462
01:25:28,457 --> 01:25:29,792
Paddle! Paddle!

1463
01:25:33,337 --> 01:25:34,713
[all screaming]

1464
01:25:34,797 --> 01:25:36,298
[bellows]

1465
01:25:36,382 --> 01:25:39,426
[roaring]

1466
01:25:44,265 --> 01:25:45,683
[gasping]

1467
01:25:45,766 --> 01:25:48,102
Isabella! Swim! Go! Go!

1468
01:25:48,978 --> 01:25:50,062
Bella!

1469
01:25:51,355 --> 01:25:52,731
[roaring]

1470
01:25:52,815 --> 01:25:54,567
Isabella! [grunts]

1471
01:26:02,241 --> 01:26:03,701
[bellows]

1472
01:26:03,784 --> 01:26:05,035
[inhales deeply]

1473
01:26:08,163 --> 01:26:10,082
[growling]

1474
01:26:21,719 --> 01:26:23,095
[roaring]

1475
01:26:24,388 --> 01:26:25,723
- Bella!
- Dad!

1476
01:26:25,806 --> 01:26:26,891
Keep going.

1477
01:26:27,641 --> 01:26:29,977
[roaring]

1478
01:26:30,060 --> 01:26:31,103
[Reuben] Come on!

1479
01:26:35,774 --> 01:26:36,942
Bella!

1480
01:26:37,985 --> 01:26:38,819
- Bella!
- Bella!

1481
01:26:38,903 --> 01:26:39,904
Isabella!

1482
01:26:39,987 --> 01:26:41,363
[grunting]

1483
01:26:41,989 --> 01:26:44,491
[Isabella, muffled] Dad! Dad!

1484
01:26:45,868 --> 01:26:46,994
Dad!

1485
01:26:47,077 --> 01:26:48,329
Bella! Bella!

1486
01:26:48,412 --> 01:26:50,331
- [roaring]
- [Isabella] Dad!

1487
01:26:50,414 --> 01:26:52,082
Dad, help!

1488
01:26:52,166 --> 01:26:54,543
[whimpering]

1489
01:26:58,422 --> 01:26:59,798
Dad!

1490
01:27:01,675 --> 01:27:02,801
Dad!

1491
01:27:02,885 --> 01:27:04,303
- No. Isabella!
- Dad!

1492
01:27:04,386 --> 01:27:06,138
[grunting]

1493
01:27:09,683 --> 01:27:11,810
No! Bella!

1494
01:27:13,896 --> 01:27:16,774
- [whimpering]
- [growling]

1495
01:27:23,197 --> 01:27:24,198
Dad.

1496
01:27:26,075 --> 01:27:27,701
[roaring]

1497
01:27:29,578 --> 01:27:30,913
Isabella, no!

1498
01:27:30,996 --> 01:27:33,582
[crying] No! No!

1499
01:27:33,666 --> 01:27:35,292
- No!
- [whimpering]

1500
01:27:41,006 --> 01:27:43,342
[panting]

1501
01:27:44,677 --> 01:27:46,720
Bella! Bella, come on. Come on! Swim!

1502
01:27:46,804 --> 01:27:47,805
Come on.

1503
01:27:48,389 --> 01:27:50,099
[roaring]

1504
01:27:54,103 --> 01:27:55,104
Dad!

1505
01:27:55,813 --> 01:27:56,939
Dad!

1506
01:27:57,022 --> 01:27:58,023
Bella, swim!

1507
01:27:59,608 --> 01:28:01,648
- [Reuben] Come on! I got you!
- Bella, it's coming!

1508
01:28:02,486 --> 01:28:03,486
- Come on!
- I got you.

1509
01:28:05,155 --> 01:28:07,366
- [roaring]
- [screaming]

1510
01:28:10,703 --> 01:28:12,121
[screaming]

1511
01:28:15,499 --> 01:28:18,460
[roaring]

1512
01:28:19,753 --> 01:28:20,838
You okay?

1513
01:28:23,590 --> 01:28:25,217
[all breathing heavily]

1514
01:28:26,552 --> 01:28:28,303
- You okay? You okay?
- [whimpers]

1515
01:28:31,140 --> 01:28:32,641
[all gasping]

1516
01:28:35,853 --> 01:28:38,355
[all laughing]

1517
01:28:44,278 --> 01:28:46,905
[hopeful music playing]

1518
01:28:56,373 --> 01:28:58,000
[birds chirping]

1519
01:29:05,090 --> 01:29:06,091
Okay.

1520
01:29:07,593 --> 01:29:09,386
It's a vertical slab.

1521
01:29:10,637 --> 01:29:11,847
Uh...

1522
01:29:12,681 --> 01:29:14,975
Looks like some overhang.

1523
01:29:16,643 --> 01:29:20,939
It's probably a 5.13, 5.15.

1524
01:29:21,440 --> 01:29:23,358
Well, at least we're going down.

1525
01:29:24,568 --> 01:29:27,780
I saw the pterosaur
circling a carved structure

1526
01:29:27,863 --> 01:29:29,698
cut into a natural ledge.

1527
01:29:30,699 --> 01:29:33,118
They were crevice-nesters,
so it's an ideal spot.

1528
01:29:36,038 --> 01:29:38,040
Do we have to get a sample from an egg?

1529
01:29:38,957 --> 01:29:40,685
I suppose we could try
and get it from the parent,

1530
01:29:40,709 --> 01:29:43,087
but they're a flying carnivore
the size of an F-16.

1531
01:29:43,170 --> 01:29:44,588
Hmm. Okay.

1532
01:29:45,172 --> 01:29:46,173
[Kincaid] Clip him in.

1533
01:29:54,681 --> 01:29:55,849
Okay. [sighs]

1534
01:29:57,935 --> 01:29:59,937
[in French]

1535
01:30:08,529 --> 01:30:09,947
- You good?
- Good?

1536
01:30:10,030 --> 01:30:11,031
Yeah, good. Yeah.

1537
01:30:11,115 --> 01:30:12,991
Yeah, yeah. Ah.

1538
01:30:13,534 --> 01:30:17,079
I mean, I have a 20-foot climbing wall
in my gym, so.

1539
01:30:17,871 --> 01:30:19,671
There you go.
It's exactly like climbing that.

1540
01:30:20,290 --> 01:30:22,501
Yeah, but 25 times in a row.

1541
01:30:22,584 --> 01:30:25,629
And you're 500 feet up, so, if you fall,

1542
01:30:26,547 --> 01:30:28,048
you die.

1543
01:30:28,132 --> 01:30:29,675
Okay. Well, that's unhelpful.

1544
01:30:30,175 --> 01:30:33,011
[chuckles] Don't worry. I got you.

1545
01:30:38,100 --> 01:30:39,434
[grunts]

1546
01:30:39,518 --> 01:30:41,812
[breathing deeply]

1547
01:30:50,529 --> 01:30:53,157
[tense music playing]

1548
01:31:04,501 --> 01:31:06,253
[grunting]

1549
01:31:06,336 --> 01:31:08,630
[panting]

1550
01:31:13,051 --> 01:31:14,219
It's fun, right?

1551
01:31:21,393 --> 01:31:23,604
[dinosaur chittering]

1552
01:31:33,030 --> 01:31:34,072
Krebs.

1553
01:31:35,991 --> 01:31:36,992
Check this out.

1554
01:31:37,075 --> 01:31:38,660
[breathing heavily]

1555
01:31:38,744 --> 01:31:39,744
- [screams]
- Rope, rope!

1556
01:31:41,371 --> 01:31:43,373
[grunting]

1557
01:31:45,584 --> 01:31:46,710
You're doing great.

1558
01:31:47,961 --> 01:31:49,546
We got another 40 feet to go.

1559
01:31:49,630 --> 01:31:52,007
[dinosaur roaring in distance]

1560
01:31:54,176 --> 01:31:55,177
Time to move.

1561
01:31:57,346 --> 01:31:58,555
Okay.

1562
01:32:06,813 --> 01:32:08,065
[grunts]

1563
01:32:08,565 --> 01:32:10,734
See? It's just like the gym.

1564
01:32:11,318 --> 01:32:12,986
[laughs softly]

1565
01:32:23,997 --> 01:32:25,707
<i>C'est miraculeux.</i>

1566
01:32:27,209 --> 01:32:29,253
Can you move the pitons?

1567
01:32:29,878 --> 01:32:32,256
I think we're gonna need
a couple minutes here.

1568
01:32:32,339 --> 01:32:33,507
[LeClerc] Okay, <i>d'accord.</i>

1569
01:32:37,261 --> 01:32:38,804
[Zora] What is this place?

1570
01:32:39,513 --> 01:32:41,014
Some kind of ancient temple?

1571
01:32:47,271 --> 01:32:48,981
It won't hurt the embryo.

1572
01:32:49,481 --> 01:32:53,193
Um, maybe we should make this quick, huh?

1573
01:32:56,071 --> 01:32:57,864
Not without breaking the egg sac.

1574
01:33:01,034 --> 01:33:03,078
You're a very impressive nerd, Henry.

1575
01:33:03,161 --> 01:33:04,830
[chuckles]

1576
01:33:08,333 --> 01:33:11,211
- What would the alternative be?
- To what?

1577
01:33:11,295 --> 01:33:14,131
To handing the samples over
to ParkerGenix?

1578
01:33:17,718 --> 01:33:18,927
We open-source it.

1579
01:33:19,970 --> 01:33:21,430
We give it to the whole world.

1580
01:33:23,015 --> 01:33:24,891
A bunch of people create the medicine,

1581
01:33:24,975 --> 01:33:27,352
nobody owns the patent,
everyone has access,

1582
01:33:27,436 --> 01:33:29,730
and tens of millions of lives are saved.

1583
01:33:29,813 --> 01:33:32,149
It's all of us, not some of us.

1584
01:33:36,278 --> 01:33:38,196
I don't make any money in that scenario.

1585
01:33:38,989 --> 01:33:40,282
Oh, no, you're broke as hell.

1586
01:33:40,866 --> 01:33:42,284
Yeah, I don't love that part.

1587
01:33:43,201 --> 01:33:44,703
[chuckles]

1588
01:33:45,996 --> 01:33:49,124
[dinosaurs snarling in distance]

1589
01:33:51,918 --> 01:33:54,504
[dinosaurs snarling in distance]

1590
01:34:05,474 --> 01:34:07,017
[chitters]

1591
01:34:07,100 --> 01:34:09,144
[squawks]

1592
01:34:11,480 --> 01:34:12,481
[gasps]

1593
01:34:12,564 --> 01:34:14,274
- [dart beeps]
- I've got it.

1594
01:34:14,358 --> 01:34:15,817
[LeClerc] Hey, Zora! <i>Attention!</i>

1595
01:34:15,901 --> 01:34:17,110
[dinosaur chirping]

1596
01:34:17,194 --> 01:34:18,779
[chittering]

1597
01:34:18,862 --> 01:34:19,863
Uh-oh.

1598
01:34:20,364 --> 01:34:21,490
[squawks]

1599
01:34:22,866 --> 01:34:24,659
[roars]

1600
01:34:24,743 --> 01:34:25,786
[grunts]

1601
01:34:26,370 --> 01:34:27,371
[Zora] Hey!

1602
01:34:32,542 --> 01:34:33,668
Hey!

1603
01:34:34,211 --> 01:34:35,629
[squawks]

1604
01:34:35,712 --> 01:34:37,672
Oh, gee. [grunts]

1605
01:34:39,800 --> 01:34:40,842
The sample!

1606
01:34:45,013 --> 01:34:46,473
[growling]

1607
01:34:47,224 --> 01:34:48,767
[grunts] Hey!

1608
01:34:52,771 --> 01:34:53,772
[grunts]

1609
01:34:54,898 --> 01:34:56,400
[squawks]

1610
01:34:56,483 --> 01:34:58,485
[growling]

1611
01:35:00,695 --> 01:35:02,614
[groans] Oh!

1612
01:35:02,697 --> 01:35:04,616
Kincaid! Pull me up!

1613
01:35:04,699 --> 01:35:06,701
- Kincaid! Help!
- LeClerc?

1614
01:35:06,785 --> 01:35:08,245
[dinosaur roaring in distance]

1615
01:35:08,328 --> 01:35:09,496
Hey!

1616
01:35:09,579 --> 01:35:11,790
[groans] Kincaid! <i>Au secours!</i>

1617
01:35:11,873 --> 01:35:14,334
Krebs, pull! Pull!

1618
01:35:14,418 --> 01:35:16,461
Pull me up! Pull me up!

1619
01:35:17,170 --> 01:35:19,631
Pull me up! Pull me up!

1620
01:35:24,344 --> 01:35:26,346
- You okay?
- Yeah. You?

1621
01:35:27,973 --> 01:35:28,807
Oh, shit.

1622
01:35:28,890 --> 01:35:30,475
[sample clatters]

1623
01:35:30,559 --> 01:35:31,559
Oh! Quick!

1624
01:35:33,353 --> 01:35:34,604
[yells]

1625
01:35:41,945 --> 01:35:43,280
[grunting]

1626
01:35:44,865 --> 01:35:46,158
[grunting]

1627
01:35:49,369 --> 01:35:51,663
Henry! [grunting]

1628
01:35:55,208 --> 01:35:56,376
[grunts]

1629
01:35:57,544 --> 01:35:58,837
[dinosaur squawks in distance]

1630
01:35:59,713 --> 01:36:01,381
[Kincaid] Pull! Up!

1631
01:36:02,340 --> 01:36:03,592
Pull me up! [screams]

1632
01:36:04,176 --> 01:36:05,302
Pull me! Pull me!

1633
01:36:07,512 --> 01:36:08,513
Kincaid!

1634
01:36:09,598 --> 01:36:11,266
[Zora] Henry! Henry, stop!

1635
01:36:11,349 --> 01:36:12,642
I nearly got it!

1636
01:36:12,726 --> 01:36:15,145
Henry, it's gonna snap! Stop! [grunts]

1637
01:36:16,605 --> 01:36:17,689
Hold on!

1638
01:36:17,772 --> 01:36:18,773
- Pull!
- [grunts]

1639
01:36:18,857 --> 01:36:21,818
- Pull! Pull!
- [growling]

1640
01:36:24,321 --> 01:36:25,489
Oh, shit!

1641
01:36:26,740 --> 01:36:28,033
Oh, shit!

1642
01:36:28,116 --> 01:36:28,950
LeClerc!

1643
01:36:29,034 --> 01:36:30,202
[screams]

1644
01:36:34,247 --> 01:36:35,957
[squawking]

1645
01:36:43,381 --> 01:36:44,591
[Krebs yells]

1646
01:36:46,968 --> 01:36:48,053
Come on! Here!

1647
01:36:48,136 --> 01:36:49,137
[Krebs] Where?

1648
01:36:49,221 --> 01:36:51,014
- [bellows]
- [grunting]

1649
01:36:56,770 --> 01:36:59,189
[both grunting]

1650
01:37:01,650 --> 01:37:04,152
Stop! Henry! It's gonna split!

1651
01:37:05,529 --> 01:37:07,197
- I've got it!
- [dart beeping]

1652
01:37:09,324 --> 01:37:10,367
[screams, grunts]

1653
01:37:12,911 --> 01:37:14,204
Oh, no.

1654
01:37:14,287 --> 01:37:16,164
Henry! Henry!

1655
01:37:17,457 --> 01:37:18,750
[yells]

1656
01:37:20,919 --> 01:37:23,296
[grunting]

1657
01:37:25,131 --> 01:37:27,092
[dinosaurs squeaking]

1658
01:37:36,393 --> 01:37:37,602
[gasping]

1659
01:37:42,691 --> 01:37:45,110
[hopeful music playing]

1660
01:37:49,906 --> 01:37:50,991
[Kincaid] Doc!

1661
01:37:51,074 --> 01:37:52,158
You okay?

1662
01:37:53,618 --> 01:37:55,120
[panting]

1663
01:38:03,837 --> 01:38:05,088
[sighs]

1664
01:38:11,386 --> 01:38:12,512
Let's go home.

1665
01:38:17,934 --> 01:38:19,269
[breathing heavily]

1666
01:38:25,483 --> 01:38:28,486
- [animals calling in distance]
- [insects trilling]

1667
01:38:49,090 --> 01:38:51,092
[animals chittering in distance]

1668
01:39:10,111 --> 01:39:12,906
[animals chirping, calling]

1669
01:39:22,707 --> 01:39:23,833
Isabella.

1670
01:39:24,709 --> 01:39:25,752
Honey.

1671
01:39:25,835 --> 01:39:26,836
Let's go.

1672
01:39:28,505 --> 01:39:29,839
We're almost there.

1673
01:39:58,118 --> 01:39:59,327
[Kincaid] I see the helipad.

1674
01:40:00,245 --> 01:40:01,788
It's in the middle of a reservoir.

1675
01:40:03,790 --> 01:40:05,291
No sign of the kids, though.

1676
01:40:06,042 --> 01:40:08,128
Will they even be able to find this place?

1677
01:40:08,753 --> 01:40:09,754
[Zora] Hey.

1678
01:40:10,839 --> 01:40:12,382
Hey, there's footprints here.

1679
01:40:13,341 --> 01:40:14,426
Hello?

1680
01:40:15,051 --> 01:40:16,219
Reuben.

1681
01:40:16,302 --> 01:40:18,012
- Teresa.
- Zora, they could be anything.

1682
01:40:18,096 --> 01:40:20,515
- They could be animals. It could be...
- In Nikes?

1683
01:40:20,598 --> 01:40:22,284
I appreciate what you're doing.
I really do.

1684
01:40:22,308 --> 01:40:23,994
- But we are in a crunch right now.
- Hello?

1685
01:40:24,018 --> 01:40:25,979
And we don't even know
if they survived the rocks.

1686
01:40:26,062 --> 01:40:28,064
- Reuben. Teresa.
- The helicopter's coming.

1687
01:40:28,148 --> 01:40:29,428
Well, we'll just have the pilots

1688
01:40:29,482 --> 01:40:31,627
do a low pass over the whole island
while there's still light.

1689
01:40:31,651 --> 01:40:34,696
- That is an incredibly dangerous idea.
- Well, nobody asked you.

1690
01:40:35,780 --> 01:40:36,781
Hello?

1691
01:40:37,282 --> 01:40:38,533
[Kincaid] Isabella!

1692
01:40:39,659 --> 01:40:40,702
Hello?

1693
01:40:41,745 --> 01:40:42,829
[Xavier] Hello?

1694
01:40:43,413 --> 01:40:44,706
Hello?

1695
01:40:46,499 --> 01:40:48,126
Anyone, please!

1696
01:40:50,545 --> 01:40:51,838
Hello?

1697
01:40:51,921 --> 01:40:53,673
[shout echoing]

1698
01:40:55,884 --> 01:40:57,302
[Reuben] Hello?

1699
01:40:57,385 --> 01:40:59,220
[Teresa] Is anyone here?

1700
01:41:00,346 --> 01:41:01,389
[Zora] Hey!

1701
01:41:01,473 --> 01:41:03,516
Oh, my God! You made it!

1702
01:41:03,600 --> 01:41:05,435
- Oh, God. Oh, my God.
- Hi.

1703
01:41:05,518 --> 01:41:06,394
You okay?

1704
01:41:06,478 --> 01:41:07,663
- Thank God you're alive.
- Where is he?

1705
01:41:07,687 --> 01:41:10,440
- No, no, no. Where is that son of a bitch?
- What? Who?

1706
01:41:10,523 --> 01:41:12,668
- That guy! He let me fall.
- Yeah, he tried to kill her.

1707
01:41:12,692 --> 01:41:14,277
Wait, wait. Who let you fall?

1708
01:41:16,946 --> 01:41:18,448
Hey, Krebs.

1709
01:41:21,534 --> 01:41:23,095
- I'm gonna kill him!
- [Kincaid] Hey, hey.

1710
01:41:23,119 --> 01:41:25,139
- [Reuben] He's got a gun, Teresa! No!
- Whoa, whoa, whoa.

1711
01:41:25,163 --> 01:41:26,763
Let's just settle down, everybody. Okay?

1712
01:41:29,000 --> 01:41:30,640
[Teresa] You stopped me calling for help.

1713
01:41:30,960 --> 01:41:32,253
You wanted me to die.

1714
01:41:32,337 --> 01:41:34,339
What are you talking about?
I tried to catch you.

1715
01:41:34,422 --> 01:41:38,009
[scoffs] No, no, no. You looked
right at me and you let me fall!

1716
01:41:38,802 --> 01:41:39,803
She's lying.

1717
01:41:39,886 --> 01:41:41,095
[scoffs]

1718
01:41:41,179 --> 01:41:42,531
If she's lying,
then why do you have a gun?

1719
01:41:42,555 --> 01:41:44,098
Because she's hysterical.

1720
01:41:44,182 --> 01:41:45,433
[Teresa chuckles]

1721
01:41:45,517 --> 01:41:46,994
No, I'm not hysterical. I'm homicidal!

1722
01:41:47,018 --> 01:41:48,204
- Hey, whoa, whoa.
- Teresa, no!

1723
01:41:48,228 --> 01:41:49,705
[Kincaid] Whoa, whoa, whoa. Hey, hey.

1724
01:41:49,729 --> 01:41:51,248
- Don't rush the guy with the gun.
- Enough.

1725
01:41:51,272 --> 01:41:53,691
Whoa. All right. All right.

1726
01:41:53,775 --> 01:41:55,610
Yes, everybody just relax.

1727
01:41:55,693 --> 01:41:57,111
Why don't I take that?

1728
01:41:57,821 --> 01:41:58,988
It looks heavy.

1729
01:42:03,660 --> 01:42:04,702
And...

1730
01:42:05,495 --> 01:42:07,956
I'll keep it safe with me.

1731
01:42:08,039 --> 01:42:10,416
- [clears throat]
- [handcuffs click]

1732
01:42:10,500 --> 01:42:12,544
Now, we're all just gonna relax

1733
01:42:12,627 --> 01:42:15,296
and wait here
for the helicopter to arrive.

1734
01:42:16,089 --> 01:42:18,007
There's a helicopter coming?

1735
01:42:18,091 --> 01:42:19,819
Any minute.
We just gotta make sure they see us.

1736
01:42:19,843 --> 01:42:21,803
[Reuben] Told you we'd made it.

1737
01:42:21,886 --> 01:42:23,429
Then we all get on the helicopter

1738
01:42:23,513 --> 01:42:26,933
and we forget any ridiculous stories
about whatever she said.

1739
01:42:27,016 --> 01:42:29,811
- [Zora] I think we all need to...
- Stop talking! Stop talking.

1740
01:42:31,312 --> 01:42:34,148
[mechanical creaking in distance]

1741
01:42:36,317 --> 01:42:39,070
[mechanical whirring]

1742
01:42:40,363 --> 01:42:41,406
It's the generator.

1743
01:42:42,657 --> 01:42:43,950
It must be on a timer.

1744
01:42:44,993 --> 01:42:47,412
[dinosaur roars]

1745
01:42:49,038 --> 01:42:50,498
The generator sounds angry.

1746
01:42:51,165 --> 01:42:53,751
Maybe this place is deserted
in the daytime.

1747
01:42:54,377 --> 01:42:56,129
But not so deserted at night?

1748
01:42:56,212 --> 01:42:57,755
[electricity powering up]

1749
01:42:57,839 --> 01:43:00,341
["Stand by Me" playing on speakers]

1750
01:43:02,927 --> 01:43:05,430
[echoing] ♪ <i>And the land is dark ♪</i>

1751
01:43:05,513 --> 01:43:09,225
<i>♪ And the moon is the only ♪</i>

1752
01:43:09,309 --> 01:43:11,519
<i>♪ Light we'll see ♪</i>

1753
01:43:13,062 --> 01:43:15,398
It's okay, honey. It's okay.

1754
01:43:15,481 --> 01:43:17,191
<i>♪ Be afraid ♪</i>

1755
01:43:17,275 --> 01:43:21,696
<i>♪ Oh, I won't be afraid ♪</i>

1756
01:43:21,779 --> 01:43:23,531
<i>♪ Just as long ♪</i>

1757
01:43:23,615 --> 01:43:25,825
<i>♪ As you stand ♪</i>

1758
01:43:25,909 --> 01:43:27,493
<i>♪ Stand by me... ♪</i>

1759
01:43:28,077 --> 01:43:29,662
[roars]

1760
01:43:30,788 --> 01:43:31,789
[squawks]

1761
01:43:31,873 --> 01:43:32,916
Oh, not that thing.

1762
01:43:32,999 --> 01:43:34,500
[snarling]

1763
01:43:37,587 --> 01:43:39,255
[roaring]

1764
01:43:40,006 --> 01:43:40,840
[Zora] Run!

1765
01:43:40,924 --> 01:43:42,842
[dramatic music playing]

1766
01:43:42,926 --> 01:43:43,927
[Zora] Split up!

1767
01:43:46,220 --> 01:43:47,597
[Teresa] Go. Go, go, go.

1768
01:43:51,768 --> 01:43:52,936
[roaring]

1769
01:43:54,395 --> 01:43:55,395
[Xavier] Reuben!

1770
01:44:01,277 --> 01:44:02,987
[Reuben] Teresa, get the rack!

1771
01:44:09,452 --> 01:44:10,453
[Teresa] What are they?

1772
01:44:10,536 --> 01:44:12,496
[Xavier] I don't know,
but they're mutant as shit!

1773
01:44:13,247 --> 01:44:14,607
[Reuben] Come on, get to the back!

1774
01:44:14,666 --> 01:44:17,126
- [banging on door]
- [Dolores chittering]

1775
01:44:26,386 --> 01:44:27,929
[snarling]

1776
01:44:33,059 --> 01:44:34,560
[banging on door]

1777
01:44:39,524 --> 01:44:42,402
- [breathing heavily]
- [electricity whirring softly]

1778
01:44:52,704 --> 01:44:54,163
The hell is this place?

1779
01:44:54,914 --> 01:44:58,042
It's a lab. Cross-breeding, mutations.

1780
01:44:59,085 --> 01:45:00,336
It's where it all happened.

1781
01:45:02,380 --> 01:45:04,215
- [snarling]
- [banging on door]

1782
01:45:07,677 --> 01:45:09,220
[panting]

1783
01:45:10,304 --> 01:45:11,806
[grunting]

1784
01:45:13,224 --> 01:45:14,600
[shrieks]

1785
01:45:15,184 --> 01:45:17,770
[Zora grunting] Stop!

1786
01:45:19,522 --> 01:45:20,690
You're missing it!

1787
01:45:20,773 --> 01:45:22,525
- Give me the gun!
- Get off me!

1788
01:45:23,443 --> 01:45:24,777
[screams]

1789
01:45:25,903 --> 01:45:28,114
[grunts, groans]

1790
01:45:30,116 --> 01:45:31,325
[vehicle creaking]

1791
01:45:36,622 --> 01:45:38,499
